{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.13.15","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":10606304}],"dockerImageVersionId":28755,"isInternetEnabled":false,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Formula 1 Podium Prediction Companion\n### CSEN903 · Milestone 1\n\n**Team:**\n\n**Goal.** For every driver entering a Formula 1 race, predict whether they finish on the podium (`positionOrder ≤ 3`).\n\n**Prediction cutoff.** After qualifying, before the race starts. Anything that only becomes known once the race is running or finished (results, status, points, laps, pit stops, updated standings) cannot be used as a feature.\n\n**Unit of analysis.** One driver entering one race, so every row of the final modeling table is one `(raceId, driverId)` pair.\n\n**Dataset.** Formula 1 World Championship (1950–2024), Kaggle / Ergast, 14 linked CSV tables.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Table of Contents\n\n1. **Data Cleaning & Auditing**\n   - 1.0 Setup & Raw Data Load\n   - Part A — Initial EDA (before cleaning)\n   - Part B — Cleaning\n   - Part C — EDA after cleaning\n2. Data-Engineering Questions\n3. Feature Selection & Engineering\n4. Predictive Modeling\n5. Experiments\n6. Model Explainability (XAI)\n7. Inference Function\n8. Conclusions & Limitations","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## The Problem and the Questions\n\nBefore each race, once qualifying is over, we want to estimate for every driver how likely they are to finish in the top three. The information available at that moment is limited to what is known before the lights go out: the starting grid, qualifying, and how the driver and their team have performed in earlier races. Everything that happens during or after the race is the outcome, not an input.\n\nTwo things make this harder than it looks. According to the F1 introduction, podiums are rare by design, about 3 of 20 drivers per race in the modern era and even fewer when fields were larger, so the positive class is small. And the dataset stores pre-race and post-race information side by side in the same tables, so the main risk is accidentally using the future to predict it.\n\nThe data therefore needs to answer three kinds of questions:\n\n1) **What is actually available before the race?** Which tables and columns describe the pre-race situation, and how complete and reliable are they across 75 seasons?\n2) **The three data-engineering questions from the brief:** front-row conversion by circuit before and after 2014, the home-race effect after controlling for grid, and mechanical retirement rates by constructor in 2014–2021 vs. 2022–2024.\n3) **How well can pre-race information predict a podium, and what does the model rely on?** This is answered by the models, experiments and explanations in Sections 4–6.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n# 1. Data Cleaning & Auditing\n\n## 1.0 Setup & Raw Data Load\n\nWe load all 14 tables exactly as they are stored in the CSV files. By default pandas silently turns some strings (such as empty cells, \"NA\" or \"null\") into missing values while leaving others untouched, which would mix its decisions with ours before we have even looked at the data. Loading with `keep_default_na=False` keeps every value as written, so deciding what counts as \"missing\" becomes a step we take and document ourselves in Part B.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nDATA_DIR = \"/kaggle/input/datasets/rohanrao/formula-1-world-championship-1950-2020/\"\n\nTABLES = [\"circuits\", \"constructors\", \"constructor_results\", \"constructor_standings\",\n          \"drivers\", \"driver_standings\", \"races\", \"results\", \"qualifying\",\n          \"lap_times\", \"pit_stops\", \"sprint_results\", \"seasons\", \"status\"]\n\n# raw tables, loaded exactly as stored. Never modified; cleaning works on copies.\nraw = {name: pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR + name + \".csv\", keep_default_na=False) for name in TABLES}\n\nfor name, df in raw.items():\n    print(f\"{name:22} {df.shape[0]:>7} rows  {df.shape[1]:>3} cols\")\n\nprint(\"\\nSeasons:\", raw[\"races\"][\"year\"].min(), \"-\", raw[\"races\"][\"year\"].max())","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:35.025937Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:35.02633Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.181556Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:35.026293Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.179633Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"circuits                    77 rows    9 cols\nconstructors               212 rows    5 cols\nconstructor_results      12625 rows    5 cols\nconstructor_standings    13391 rows    7 cols\ndrivers                    861 rows    9 cols\ndriver_standings         34863 rows    7 cols\nraces                     1125 rows   18 cols\nresults                  26759 rows   18 cols\nqualifying               10494 rows    9 cols\nlap_times               589081 rows    6 cols\npit_stops                11371 rows    7 cols\nsprint_results             360 rows   16 cols\nseasons                     75 rows    2 cols\nstatus                     139 rows    2 cols\n\nSeasons: 1950 - 2024\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Observations\n\nAll 14 tables loaded, and the races run from 1950 to 2024. The `seasons` table has 75 rows, one for each year in that range.\n\n1) **results:** has 26,759 rows across 1,125 races, about 24 entries per race, assuming every race has results, which we still need to check. The F1 introduction describes a modern race as about 20 drivers and says fields in the 1950s–1980s were much larger, with non-qualifiers still getting a result row. Our hypothesis is that the extra rows come from those older, bigger fields. If that is true, the share of podium rows should be lower in those seasons, since every race still has only three podium places. We check this season by season in the EDA.\n\n2) **qualifying:** has only 10,494 rows, about 39% of the size of `results`. At roughly 20 drivers per race, that covers only around 500 races. This fits the brief's warning about \"historical qualifying-coverage drift\" and the introduction's statement that qualifying is only recorded in detail for recent decades. We need to find out exactly which seasons it covers before deciding how to use it.\n\n3) **sprint_results:** is small (360 rows), which fits sprints only existing from 2021, a handful per season. A sprint is a short race held on the same weekend but before the main race starts, so its result is already available at our prediction cutoff and, by the brief's definition of leakage, is not future information. Whether it is useful as a feature is a separate question: it only covers a few recent races, which we look at when building features.\n\n4) **lap_times** and **pit_stops:** describe what happened during the race, so by the brief's definition they cannot be features. `lap_times` is the largest table (589,081 rows), and we still need it in cleaning, because the brief asks us to convert lap-time strings into seconds.\n\n5) **driver_standings** and **constructor_standings:** are updated after each race, so the brief counts them as post-race information. According to the introduction, they only become usable if shifted to \"standings before this race\".\n\nTwo things we want to check: whether every one of the 1,125 races actually has results, and which seasons qualifying covers. First, we open each table to see what its columns contain.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Part A — Initial EDA (before cleaning)\n\n## 1.1 Getting to Know the Tables\n\nBefore measuring anything, we want to know what each table holds. We look at the first rows of each table, describe what every column means, and note anything that will need attention in cleaning and whether its information is available before the race starts, since that decides whether it can ever be used as a feature.\n\nWe go through the tables in five groups, starting with the small tables that describe things and ending with the tables that refer to them, so that by the time we reach a table full of IDs we already know what each ID points to. How the tables actually link is verified in 1.6.\n\n| Group | Tables | What they describe |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lookups | circuits, constructors, drivers, seasons, status | The circuits, teams, drivers, years and finishing reasons, independent of any single race |\n| Calendar | races | One row per Grand Prix, which gives every race its date |\n| Race weekend sessions | qualifying, sprint_results, results | What happened in each session, in weekend order |\n| Points and standings | constructor_results, driver_standings, constructor_standings | Points scored per race and the championship position after each race |\n| Race details | lap_times, pit_stops | Lap-by-lap times and pit stops during the race |","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":" ## 1.1.1 **circuits**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"circuits\"]\ndf.info()\ndisplay(df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.185052Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.185461Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.244326Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.18542Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.243674Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nRangeIndex: 77 entries, 0 to 76\nData columns (total 9 columns):\n #   Column      Non-Null Count  Dtype  \n---  ------      --------------  -----  \n 0   circuitId   77 non-null     int64  \n 1   circuitRef  77 non-null     object \n 2   name        77 non-null     object \n 3   location    77 non-null     object \n 4   country     77 non-null     object \n 5   lat         77 non-null     float64\n 6   lng         77 non-null     float64\n 7   alt         77 non-null     int64  \n 8   url         77 non-null     object \ndtypes: float64(2), int64(2), object(5)\nmemory usage: 5.5+ KB\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"   circuitId   circuitRef                            name      location  \\\n0          1  albert_park  Albert Park Grand Prix Circuit     Melbourne   \n1          2       sepang    Sepang International Circuit  Kuala Lumpur   \n2          3      bahrain   Bahrain International Circuit        Sakhir   \n3          4    catalunya  Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya      Montmeló   \n4          5     istanbul                   Istanbul Park      Istanbul   \n\n     country       lat        lng  alt  \\\n0  Australia -37.84970  144.96800   10   \n1   Malaysia   2.76083  101.73800   18   \n2    Bahrain  26.03250   50.51060    7   \n3      Spain  41.57000    2.26111  109   \n4     Turkey  40.95170   29.40500  130   \n\n                                                 url  \n0  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melbourne_Grand_P...  \n1  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sepang_Internatio...  \n2  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahrain_Internati...  \n3  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circuit_de_Barcel...  \n4         http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Istanbul_Park  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>circuitId</th>\n      <th>circuitRef</th>\n      <th>name</th>\n      <th>location</th>\n      <th>country</th>\n      <th>lat</th>\n      <th>lng</th>\n      <th>alt</th>\n      <th>url</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>albert_park</td>\n      <td>Albert Park Grand Prix Circuit</td>\n      <td>Melbourne</td>\n      <td>Australia</td>\n      <td>-37.84970</td>\n      <td>144.96800</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melbourne_Grand_P...</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>sepang</td>\n      <td>Sepang International Circuit</td>\n      <td>Kuala Lumpur</td>\n      <td>Malaysia</td>\n      <td>2.76083</td>\n      <td>101.73800</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sepang_Internatio...</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>bahrain</td>\n      <td>Bahrain International Circuit</td>\n      <td>Sakhir</td>\n      <td>Bahrain</td>\n      <td>26.03250</td>\n      <td>50.51060</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahrain_Internati...</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>catalunya</td>\n      <td>Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya</td>\n      <td>Montmeló</td>\n      <td>Spain</td>\n      <td>41.57000</td>\n      <td>2.26111</td>\n      <td>109</td>\n      <td>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circuit_de_Barcel...</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>istanbul</td>\n      <td>Istanbul Park</td>\n      <td>Istanbul</td>\n      <td>Turkey</td>\n      <td>40.95170</td>\n      <td>29.40500</td>\n      <td>130</td>\n      <td>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Istanbul_Park</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**circuits** has one row per circuit, 77 in total, describing where races are held:\n\n- **circuitId:** the number that identifies each circuit. Other tables (we expect `races`) use it to say where a race took place.\n- **circuitRef:** a short text name for the circuit (e.g. `albert_park`), another identifier.\n- **name:** the full official name of the circuit.\n- **location:** the city or area where the circuit is (e.g. Melbourne).\n- **country:** the country of the circuit, written as a country name (e.g. Australia, Spain).\n- **lat / lng:** the circuit's geographic coordinates.\n- **alt:** the circuit's altitude, in metres, above sea level.\n- **url:** the circuit's Wikipedia page.\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **Types:** `lat` and `lng` were read as decimal numbers (`float64`), and `alt` and `circuitId` as whole numbers (`int64`). pandas stores a whole column as text (`object`) if even one value in it is not a number, such as the `\\N` placeholder. Since these four columns came out as numbers, none of them contains a placeholder. The non-null counts in `.info()` are all 77, but at this stage that does not mean nothing is missing: we loaded the data without letting pandas mark anything as missing, so a `\\N` is counted as a normal value. The audit (1.2) counts the placeholders directly.\n\n2) **country** is the column that matters most here. Data-engineering Question 2 compares races in a driver's home country with races elsewhere. In the rows shown, countries are written as names (Australia, Spain, Turkey). The F1 introduction mentions that drivers' nationality is stored differently, as an adjective such as \"British\". We check this when we reach `drivers`; if the two are written differently, we will need a mapping between them for Question 2.\n\n3) **circuitId** is needed for Question 1, which asks at which circuits a front-row start (grid 1 or 2) most often turns into a podium. To answer it, we compute that rate separately for each circuit, so every result must be linked to the circuit where the race took place, through `circuitId`.\n\n4) **Available before the race?** Yes. A circuit is a fixed fact about the race known long in advance, so nothing in this table is leakage. `lat`, `lng`, `alt`, `url` and `circuitRef` are descriptive and not needed for any of our questions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How are countries written?** The first rows only show 5 of the 77 circuits. Since Question 2 depends on matching circuit countries to driver nationalities, each country has to be written in exactly one way: if the same country appeared under two different names, it would be counted as two separate countries. We list every distinct value to check.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(df[\"country\"].nunique(), \"different country values\")\nprint(sorted(df[\"country\"].unique()))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.246228Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.246824Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.253306Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.246782Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.252274Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"35 different country values\n['Argentina', 'Australia', 'Austria', 'Azerbaijan', 'Bahrain', 'Belgium', 'Brazil', 'Canada', 'China', 'France', 'Germany', 'Hungary', 'India', 'Italy', 'Japan', 'Korea', 'Malaysia', 'Mexico', 'Monaco', 'Morocco', 'Netherlands', 'Portugal', 'Qatar', 'Russia', 'Saudi Arabia', 'Singapore', 'South Africa', 'Spain', 'Sweden', 'Switzerland', 'Turkey', 'UAE', 'UK', 'USA', 'United States']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"There are 35 distinct country values, and the United States appears under two labels: `USA` and `United States`. Grouping by country as it stands would split the US into two countries. Most countries are written as full names, but a few use short forms (`UK`, `UAE`). `UK` is also the form the F1 introduction uses as its example. We look at which circuits carry each of the two US labels.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df[df[\"country\"].isin([\"USA\", \"United States\"])][[\"circuitId\", \"name\", \"location\", \"country\"]]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.2548Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.255165Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.281844Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.255127Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.280805Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":4,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"    circuitId                             name        location        country\n18         19      Indianapolis Motor Speedway    Indianapolis            USA\n22         80   Las Vegas Strip Street Circuit       Las Vegas  United States\n32         33           Phoenix street circuit         Phoenix            USA\n36         37           Detroit Street Circuit         Detroit            USA\n41         42                        Fair Park          Dallas            USA\n42         43                       Long Beach      California            USA\n43         44         Las Vegas Street Circuit          Nevada            USA\n45         46                     Watkins Glen  New York State            USA\n59         60  Riverside International Raceway      California            USA\n62         63    Sebring International Raceway         Florida            USA\n68         69          Circuit of the Americas          Austin            USA\n76         79    Miami International Autodrome           Miami            USA","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>circuitId</th>\n      <th>name</th>\n      <th>location</th>\n      <th>country</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>18</th>\n      <td>19</td>\n      <td>Indianapolis Motor Speedway</td>\n      <td>Indianapolis</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>22</th>\n      <td>80</td>\n      <td>Las Vegas Strip Street Circuit</td>\n      <td>Las Vegas</td>\n      <td>United States</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>32</th>\n      <td>33</td>\n      <td>Phoenix street circuit</td>\n      <td>Phoenix</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>36</th>\n      <td>37</td>\n      <td>Detroit Street Circuit</td>\n      <td>Detroit</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>41</th>\n      <td>42</td>\n      <td>Fair Park</td>\n      <td>Dallas</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>42</th>\n      <td>43</td>\n      <td>Long Beach</td>\n      <td>California</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>43</th>\n      <td>44</td>\n      <td>Las Vegas Street Circuit</td>\n      <td>Nevada</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>45</th>\n      <td>46</td>\n      <td>Watkins Glen</td>\n      <td>New York State</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>59</th>\n      <td>60</td>\n      <td>Riverside International Raceway</td>\n      <td>California</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>62</th>\n      <td>63</td>\n      <td>Sebring International Raceway</td>\n      <td>Florida</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>68</th>\n      <td>69</td>\n      <td>Circuit of the Americas</td>\n      <td>Austin</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>76</th>\n      <td>79</td>\n      <td>Miami International Autodrome</td>\n      <td>Miami</td>\n      <td>USA</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The US has 12 circuits in the data. Eleven are labelled `USA`; only one, the Las Vegas Strip Street Circuit (circuitId 80), is labelled `United States`. The inconsistency therefore comes from a single circuit, and in cleaning (1.8) we relabel it as `USA`, the form used by all the other US circuits. Without this, Question 2 would treat races at this circuit as being in a different country from every other US race.\n\nThe `location` column is not consistent either: for some circuits it gives a city (Detroit, Austin, Miami), for others a whole state (California, Nevada, Florida). This does not affect our work. Question 1 identifies circuits by `circuitId` and Question 2 matches drivers to circuits by `country`, so `location` is not used anywhere and we leave it as it is.\n\nThe list also includes the Indianapolis Motor Speedway (circuitId 19), home of the Indianapolis 500. According to the F1 introduction, this American race counted in the F1 championship from 1950 to 1960 even though almost no regular F1 drivers took part, which would distort Question 2 on home races, so excluding it is a defensible choice. The races themselves are listed in the `races` table, so we come back to this there.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.1.2 **constructors**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"constructors\"]\ndf.info()\ndisplay(df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.28303Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.284091Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.313918Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.284049Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:36.312908Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nRangeIndex: 212 entries, 0 to 211\nData columns (total 5 columns):\n #   Column          Non-Null Count  Dtype \n---  ------          --------------  ----- \n 0   constructorId   212 non-nul