longs to (points to `constructors`).\n- **points:** the points the team scored in that race.\n- **status:** every value in the first rows is `\\N`; we cannot tell yet what this column is meant to hold.\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **Types:** the three ID columns are whole numbers and `points` is a decimal number, so none of them contains a placeholder. `status` is text, and its first five values are all `\\N`.\n\n2) **Available before the race?** No. The points a team scored in a race are part of that race's result, so using them for the same race would be leakage. They only become usable as information about earlier races, for example to measure a team's recent form. We decide in Section 3 whether to build constructor form from this table or from another one.\n\n3) **One row per team per race** is what the first rows suggest, but we have only seen five rows of one race. The key audit (1.6) checks that every row has its own ID. Whether each `(raceId, constructorId)` pair appears only once matters only if we build constructor form from this table, so we check it if we do.\n\n**What does `status` hold?** All five values shown are `\\N`, so we count every value in the column.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df[\"status\"].value_counts()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.213137Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.213628Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.237763Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.213588Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.236696Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":25,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"status\n\\N    12608\nD        17\nName: count, dtype: int64"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":25},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`status` is empty (`\\N`) in 12,608 of the 12,625 rows; only 17 rows have a value, the letter `D`. In `results`, `D` was the code for disqualified, so these are most likely races where a team's result was 