th>\n      <td>841</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1:32.342</td>\n      <td>92342</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":29},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`lap_times` has 589,081 rows, the largest table. From the first rows, each row is one lap driven by one driver in one race:\n\n- **raceId / driverId:** the race and the driver the row belongs to.\n- **lap:** the lap number.\n- **position:** the driver's position in the race at the end of that lap.\n- **time:** the lap time, written as minutes:seconds (e.g. `1:38.109`).\n- **milliseconds:** the same lap time as one number (98,109 ms).\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **No ID column:** unlike most tables, there is no column that numbers the rows. A row is identified by the combination of race, driver and lap, which we check in the key audit (1.6).\n\n2) **Types and a built-in check:** every column except `time` is a whole number, so none of them contains a placeholder; in particular every lap has a numeric `milliseconds`. `time` holds the same value as text: `1:38.109` is 60 + 38.109 = 98.109 seconds, exactly the 98,109 ms next to it. The brief asks us to convert lap-time strings like this into seconds, and `milliseconds` lets us verify that our conversion is correct.\n\n3) **Available before the race?** No. Lap times and positions during the race describe the race itself, and the brief explicitly lists laps as not allowed as features. We do not use this table for features or for the three questions; its only role is the time conversion in cleaning.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.1.14 **pit_stops**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"pit_stops\"]\ndf.info()\ndisplay(df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.433938Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.434321Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.456808Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.434293Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.455135Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nRangeIndex: 11371 entries, 0 to 11370\nData columns (total 7 columns):\n #   Column        Non-Null Count  Dtype \n---  ------        --------------  ----- \n 0   raceId        11371 non-null  int64 \n 1   driverId      11371 non-null  int64 \n 2   stop          11371 non-null  int64 \n 3   lap           11371 non-null  int64 \n 4   time          11371 non-null  object\n 5   duration      11371 non-null  object\n 6   milliseconds  11371 non-null  int64 \ndtypes: int64(5), object(2)\nmemory usage: 622.0+ KB\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"   raceId  driverId  stop  lap      time duration  milliseconds\n0     841       153     1    1  17:05:23   26.898         26898\n1     841        30     1    1  17:05:52   25.021         25021\n2     841        17     1   11  17:20:48   23.426         23426\n3     841         4     1   12  17:22:34   23.251         23251\n4     841        13     1   13  17:24:10   23.842         23842","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>raceId</th>\n      <th>driverId</th>\n      <th>stop</th>\n      <th>lap</th>\n      <th>time</th>\n      <th>duration</th>\n      <th>milliseconds</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>841</td>\n      <td>153</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>17:05:23</td>\n      <td>26.898</td>\n      <td>26898</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>841</td>\n      <td>30</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>17:05:52</td>\n      <td>25.021</td>\n      <td>25021</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>841</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>17:20:48</td>\n      <td>23.426</td>\n      <td>23426</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>841</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>17:22:34</td>\n      <td>23.251</td>\n      <td>23251</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>841</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>17:24:10</td>\n      <td>23.842</td>\n      <td>23842</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":30},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`pit_stops` has 11,371 rows. From the first rows, each row is one pit stop made by one driver in one race:\n\n- **raceId / driverId:** the race and the driver the row belongs to.\n- **stop:** which stop it was for that driver (1 = first stop).\n- **lap:** the lap on which the stop was made.\n- **time:** the time of day when the stop happened (e.g. `17:05:23`).\n- **duration:** how long the stop took, in seconds, written as text (`26.898`).\n- **milliseconds:** the same duration as one number (26,898 ms).\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **No ID column:** like `lap_times`, a row is identified by a combination, here race, driver and stop number, which we check in the key audit (1.6).\n\n2) **\"time\" means something different in every table:** here it is a time of day, while in `results` it was a total race time or a gap to the winner, and in `qualifying` and `lap_times` a lap time. Columns with the same name therefore cannot all be converted the same way in cleaning; each has to be typed by what it means.\n\n3) **Types:** `duration` looks like a plain number of seconds but is stored as text, so at least one value somewhere is not a plain number; the audit will show it. `milliseconds` holds the same information as a whole number for every row.\n\n4) **Available before the race?** No. Pit stops happen during the race, and the brief explicitly lists them as not allowed as features. We do not use this table for features or for the three questions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Summary of 1.1\n\nWe explored all 14 tables. The table below summarises what each one holds, whether its information is known before the race starts, and what we use it for.\n\n| Table | What it holds | Known before the race? | What we use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| circuits | One row per circuit | Yes | Q1 (by circuit), Q2 (circuit country) |\n| constructors | One row per team | Yes | Q3 and constructor features, always by `constructorId` |\n| drivers | One row per driver | Yes | Q2 (nationality); date of birth as a possible age feature |\n| seasons | One row per year | Yes | Consistency check only (agrees with `races`) |\n| status | Reasons a race ended | No | Q3 (mechanical retirements), identifying non-starters |\n| races | One row per race: year, round, date, circuit | Yes | Time order, eras, data split, link to circuits |\n| qualifying | Qualifying position and lap times | Yes (our cutoff) | Features, Q1 (qualifying vs grid); covers only 494 of 1,125 races |\n| sprint_results | Sprint race results | Yes | Not used: only 18 races, all from 2021 |\n| results | Each driver's race: grid, finish, points, status | Only the IDs and `grid` | Target (`positionOrder ≤ 3`), grid feature, past races for form |\n| constructor_results | Team points per race | Only for earlier races | Possible constructor form |\n| driver_standings | Driver championship after each race | Only if shifted to the previous race | Driver form feature |\n| constructor_standings | Team championship after each race | Only if shifted to the previous race | Constructor form feature; none before 1958 |\n| lap_times | Every lap of every driver | No | Not used (time conversion check only) |\n| pit_stops | Every pit stop | No | Not used |\n\n**What we carry into the next steps**\n\n1) **Placeholders:** missing values are written as `\\N`, and in some columns as empty text; both must be treated as missing.\n2) **Time order:** neither row order nor IDs follow time, so anything based on earlier races must be sorted by `date` (or `year` and `round`).\n3) **Leakage:** standings and result columns describe the race they belong to, so they can only be used from earlier races.\n4) **Joining:** `qualifying` and `results` are combined on `raceId` and `driverId` only, and a driver's team is taken from `results`.\n5) **Fixes for cleaning:** `United States` → `USA`, `Argentinian ` → `Argentine`.\n6) **Rows to decide on:** duplicated drivers in the same race (shared drives), drivers who never started, and the Indianapolis 500 (to be selected by name, not by circuit).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.2 Pre-Cleaning Audit\n\nIn 1.1 we saw placeholders in many tables: `\\N` in most of them, and empty values in `qualifying.q2` and `q3`. Here we measure them in every column of every table at once. The audit is written as a function so that exactly the same measurement can be repeated after cleaning (Part C) and the two compared.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def audit(tables):\n    \"\"\"One row per column: how many values are \\\\N, empty, or already missing (NaN).\"\"\"\n    rows = []\n    for name, table in tables.items():\n        for col in table.columns:\n            n_sentinel = (table[col] == \"\\\\N\").sum()\n            n_empty = (table[col] == \"\").sum()\n            n_nan = table[col].isna().sum()\n            n_missing = n_sentinel + n_empty + n_nan\n            rows.append({\"table\": name, \"column\": col,\n                         \"n_sentinel\": n_sentinel, \"n_empty\": n_empty, \"n_nan\": n_nan,\n                         \"pct_missing\": round(100 * n_missing / len(table), 1)})\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\naudit_before = audit(raw)\naffected = audit_before[audit_before[\"n_sentinel\"] + audit_before[\"n_empty\"] + audit_before[\"n_nan\"] > 0]\naffected = affected.sort_values(\"pct_missing\", ascending=False)\n\nprint(\"Columns with missing values:\", len(affected), \"of\", len(audit_before))\ndisplay(affected)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.458554Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.458955Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.786261Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.458916Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.785348Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Columns with missing values: 30 of 120\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                   table           column  n_sentinel  n_empty  n_nan  \\\n18   constructor_results           status       12608        0      0   \n59                 races      sprint_time        1110        0      0   \n58                 races      sprint_date        1107        0      0   \n55                 races         fp3_time        1072        0      0   \n53                 races         fp2_time        1057        0      0   \n51                 races         fp1_time        1057        0      0   \n57                 races       quali_time        1057        0      0   \n54                 races         fp3_date        1053        0      0   \n28               drivers           number         802        0      0   \n50                 races         fp1_date        1035        0      0   \n52                 races         fp2_date        1035        0      0   \n56                 races       quali_date        1035        0      0   \n29               drivers             code         757        0      0   \n72               results     milliseconds       19079        0      0   \n71               results             time       19079        0      0   \n76               results  fastestLapSpeed       18507        0      0   \n75               results   fastest