td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>112</th>\n      <td>sprint_results</td>\n      <td>milliseconds</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>5.6</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>111</th>\n      <td>sprint_results</td>\n      <td>time</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>5.6</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>106</th>\n      <td>sprint_results</td>\n      <td>position</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>4.2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>114</th>\n      <td>sprint_results</td>\n      <td>fastestLapTime</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>2.5</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>113</th>\n      <td>sprint_results</td>\n      <td>fastestLap</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>2.5</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>84</th>\n      <td>qualifying</td>\n      <td>q1</td>\n      <td>156</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>1.5</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>64</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>number</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":31},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Where are the placeholders?** The plot shows, for every affected column, the share of rows that hold a placeholder.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"labels = affected[\"table\"] + \".\" + affected[\"column\"]\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 0.3 * len(affected) + 1))\nax.barh(labels, affected[\"pct_missing\"])\nax.invert_yaxis()\nax.set_xlabel(\"% of rows that are \\\\N or empty\")\nax.set_title(\"Placeholder values per column (raw data)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.787303Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.787596Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.170569Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:37.78757Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.169091Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 800x1000 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":32},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\nThe 30 affected columns fall into three groups, and only one of them matters for our features:\n\n1) **Columns we never use:** `constructor_results.status` is almost entirely empty (12,608 of 12,625 rows). The session schedule columns in `races` (`fp1_date` to `sprint_time`) are missing for 92–99% of races and `races.time` for 65%, and `drivers.number` and `drivers.code` are missing for most drivers (93% and 88%). None of these is needed for our questions or features, so their gaps cost us nothing.\n2) **Post-race columns:** in `results`, `time` and `milliseconds` (71%), the fastest-lap columns (68–69%) and `position` (41%) are missing for many rows, and the same kind of columns in `sprint_results` for a few. They describe the outcome of the race, so they are never features. `position` being missing so often is why the target uses `positionOrder`, which does not appear in the plot because it is complete; neither does `grid`.\n3) **Qualifying times:** `q1` is almost always filled (1.5% missing), while `q2` is missing for 44% of rows and `q3` for 65%. This is the knockout format from 1.1: a driver knocked out in Q1 or Q2 has no later time. These gaps are not errors; they tell us how far a driver got in qualifying, which we can use when building qualifying features in Section 3.\n\n**What the plot cannot show:** it measures gaps inside the rows that exist. Races with no qualifying rows at all do not appear here; we measure that coverage per season in 1.3.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.3 Pre-Cleaning EDA: F1 Over Time\n\nSeveral questions from 1.1 depend on the season: whether older races really had larger fields, which seasons have qualifying data, what a grid position of 0 means, and how often the same driver appears twice in one race. We answer each with a per-season view. All columns used here (`year`, `grid`, `positionOrder`, `positionText`) are complete, so this can be done on the raw data.\n\nFirst, we attach the season to every result.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Attach the season to every result (results -> races through raceId)\nres = raw[\"results\"].merge(raw[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\nprint(\"Rows before and after the join:\", len(raw[\"results\"]), len(res))\nprint(\"Races with results:\", res[\"raceId\"].nunique(), \"of\", len(raw[\"races\"]))\n\nres[\"podium\"] = res[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.171865Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.173031Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.189766Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.172983Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.188641Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows before and after the join: 26759 26759\nRaces with results: 1125 of 1125\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":33},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Field size and podium share.** In 1.0 we found about 24 entries per race, more than a modern grid of 20, and expected older seasons to have larger fields. Since every race has only three podium places, the share of podium rows should then be lower in those seasons.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"per_season = res.groupby(\"year\").agg(races=(\"raceId\", \"nunique\"),\n                                     rows=(\"raceId\", \"size\"),\n                                     podium_rows=(\"podium\", \"sum\"))\nper_season[\"entrants_per_race\"] = per_season[\"rows\"] / per_season[\"races\"]\nper_season[\"podium_share\"] = 100 * per_season[\"podium_rows\"] / per_season[\"rows\"]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\nper_season[\"entrants_per_race\"].plot(ax=axes[0])\naxes[0].axhline(20, linestyle=\"--\", color=\"grey\", label=\"modern grid (20)\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Average entrants per race\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"entrants\")\naxes[0].legend()\n\nper_season[\"podium_share\"].plot(ax=axes[1], color=\"darkorange\")\naxes[1].axhline(15, linestyle=\"--\", color=\"grey\", label=\"3 of 20 = 15%\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"Share of result rows that are podiums\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"%\")\naxes[1].legend()\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.191331Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.191705Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.615544Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.191675Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.614446Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":34},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Field sizes varied a lot:** the average number of entrants per race was above 20 for most of history: around 25–27 in the 1950s, 25–30 from the mid-1970s to the mid-1980s, and a peak of almost 39 around 1989. It fell below 20 only in the late 1960s (lowest, about 16, in 1969), and it has been exactly 20 in the most recent seasons.\n2) **The podium share mirrors the field size, as we expected in 1.0:** where fields are large the share is low (lowest, about 8%, around 1989), and where they are small it is high (highest, almost 19%, in 1969). Every race has three podium places, so this is exactly what larger fields should do. In recent seasons the share sits on the 15% line of a 20-car grid.\n3) **The base rate depends on the era:** depending on the season, between about 8% and 19% of rows are podiums. A model trained on one era sees a different share of podiums than it meets in another, which matters for the data split in Section 3.\n\n**What we do next:** part of these large fields are drivers who never started, which the grid-0 view below shows and 1.8 removes. 1.9 redraws this plot after cleaning.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Qualifying coverage.** In 1.1 we found qualifying data for only 494 of the 1,125 races. Here we see which seasons those races come from.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"races_per_season = raw[\"races\"].groupby(\"year\")[\"raceId\"].nunique()\nquali_races = raw[\"races\"][raw[\"races\"][\"raceId\"].isin(raw[\"qualifying\"][\"raceId\"])]\nquali_per_season = quali_races.groupby(\"year\")[\"raceId\"].nunique()\n\ncoverage = (100 * quali_per_season / races_per_season).fillna(0)\nprint(\"First season with any qualifying data:\", coverage[coverage > 0].index.min())\n\nax = coverage.plot(figsize=(10, 4))\nax.set_title(\"Share of races with qualifying data, per season\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"% of races\")\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.616624Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.61694Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.788327Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.616914Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.787377Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"First season with any qualifying data: 1994\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":35},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **No qualifying data before 1994:** for 44 seasons (1950–1993) not a single race has a qualifying record. The F1 introduction expected the table to begin in the mid-1990s, and our copy confirms 1994.\n2) **Full coverage only from 2003:** 1994 and 1995 are almost or fully covered, but coverage then collapses: between about 44% and 59% of races in 1996–1998, and below 25% in 1999–2002 (lowest about 6%, in 2001). From 2003 onward every race has qualifying data.\n3) **This is the qualifying-coverage drift the brief warns about:** a qualifying feature would be missing for every race before 1994 and for most races in 1996–2002. The gaps are not random: whether qualifying exists depends only on the season.\n\n**What this means for Section 3:** the F1 introduction gives three options: impute qualifying, fall back to `grid` (which is complete), or restrict the training window to seasons with full coverage. A window starting in 2003 would contain all three splits (train ≤ 2019, validation 2020–2021, test ≥ 2022) with complete qualifying data. We make the decision in Section 3.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What does a grid position of 0 mean?** `grid` is our main pre-race variable and is complete, but it contains the value 0, which the F1 introduction describes as a pit-lane start and asks us to verify. In 1.1 we also saw drivers who never started the race (`positionText` F for failed to qualify and W for withdrawn). We split the grid-0 rows of each season into those two groups.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"res[\"grid_zero\"] = res[\"grid\"] == 0\nres[\"never_started\"] = res[\"positionText\"].isin([\"F\", \"W\"])\nprint(\"Rows with grid = 0:\", res[\"grid_zero\"].sum(), \"of\", len(res))\n\nres[\"zero_never_started\"] = res[\"grid_zero\"] & res[\"never_started\"]\nres[\"zero_started\"] = res[\"grid_zero\"] & ~res[\"never_started\"]\ngrid_zero_share = 100 * res.groupby(\"year\")[[\"zero_never_started\", \"zero_started\"]].mean()\n\nax = grid_zero_share.plot(figsize=(10, 4))\nax.set_title(\"Share of rows with grid = 0, per season\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"% of result rows\")\nax.legend([\"grid 0, never started (F / W)\", \"grid 0, started the race\"])\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.789306Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.789574Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.989661Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.789551Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.988564Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows with grid = 0: 1638 of 26759\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":36},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Most grid-0 rows are drivers who never started:** the blue line (F or W) dominates, with peaks around 21% in the early 1960s, 17–20% around 1977 and 1982, and almost 33% in 1989. After the mid-1990s it is close to 0. In those eras a grid of 0 usually meant \"did not get a starting position\", not a pit-lane start.\n2) **Grid 0 for a driver who did start is rare:** the orange line stays at 0 until about 1980 and below 1% until the mid-2010s, then rises to about 2–4% of rows from 2019. These are the pit-lane starts the F1 introduction describes.\n3) **Grid 0 cannot be read as a number:** for a model, 0 would look better than pole position (1), when it actually means the back of the field or no start at all.\n\n**What we do next:** the non-starters are removed in 1.8. The pit-lane starts that remain are real races, so in Section 3 we recode their grid as a start from the back instead of leaving it at 0.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How often does the same driver appear twice in one race?** The brief requires one row per driver per race, and warns that early F1 breaks this through shared drives. We count the rows whose `(raceId, driverId)` pair appears more than once, per season.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"dup_rows = res[res.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], keep=False)]\nprint(\"Rows involved in duplicates:\", len(dup_rows))\nprint(\"Duplicated (race, driver) pairs:\", len(dup_rows[[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]].drop_duplicates()))\n\nax = dup_rows.groupby(\"year\").size().plot(kind=\"bar\", figsize=(10, 4))\nax.set_title(\"Rows sharing the same (raceId, driverId), per season\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"rows\")\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.990872Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.991275Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.190278Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:38.991237Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.189305Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows involved in duplicates: 176\nDuplicated (race, driver) pairs: 85\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":37},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Duplicates peak in 1953–1956:** these four seasons alone hold 120 of the 176 rows (30, 33, 33 and 24).\n2) **Almost all fall in the shared-drive era:** 158 of the 176 rows (90%) are from 1950–1957, the years in which the F1 introduction says a team could hand a car to another driver mid-race.\n3) **A few come later:** 18 rows are from 1958–1978 (6 in 1958, then 2–4 in a handful of seasons up to 1978). They come after the period the introduction describes, so they may have a different cause; we handle them with the same rule as the others.\n\n**What we do next:** in 1.7 we look at the 85 pairs in detail and choose which row to keep.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Part B — Cleaning\n\nPart A showed us what is wrong with the raw tables. In Part B we fix those problems one at a time, each in its own step. Every fix is applied to copies of the tables, so `raw` stays exactly as loaded and we can always compare against it.\n\n## 1.4 Turning placeholders into real missing values\n\nThe audit in 1.2 showed that missing values in this dataset are not real missing values. They are written as the text `\\N`, or as empty strings, like the ones we found in `q2` and `q3` in `qualifying`. pandas treats both as ordinary text, so `isna()` cannot see them, and every count of missing data would be wrong.\n\nSo we replace both with `NaN`, which is pandas' real missing value. We do this on a copy of every table, stored in `clean`.\n\nThis step only changes how missing values are written. Columns that still hold numbers or dates as text are converted in the next step.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import numpy as np\n\nclean = {}\nfor name, table in raw.items():\n    clean[name] = table.replace([\"\\\\N\", \"\"], np.nan)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.191513Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.191796Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.419513Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.191767Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.418465Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":38},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"To check that this step did what we wanted, we run the audit from 1.2 again on both versions. If the conversion worked:\n\n1) **`clean` has no placeholders left:** no `\\N` and no empty strings.\n2) **`raw` still has all of them:** this shows the original tables were not changed.\n3) **Each column's missing share is the same as in 1.2:** we only changed how \"missing\" is written, not how much is missing.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"audit_after = audit(clean)\naudit_raw_now = audit(raw)\n\nprint(\"Placeholders left in clean:\", audit_after[\"n_sentinel\"].sum() + audit_after[\"n_empty\"].sum())\nprint(\"Placeholders still in raw:\", audit_raw_now[\"n_sentinel\"].sum() + audit_raw_now[\"n_empty\"].sum())\nprint(\"Missing share per column unchanged:\", (audit_after[\"pct_missing\"] == audit_before[\"pct_missing\"]).all())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.420676Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.420992Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.945815Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.420965Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.9448Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Placeholders left in clean: 0\nPlaceholders still in raw: 160144\nMissing share per column unchanged: True\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":39},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **No placeholders are left in `clean`:** every `\\N` and empty string is now `NaN`, so from here on `isna()` counts missing values correctly.\n2) **`raw` is unchanged:** the original tables still contain all their placeholders, so the loaded data is kept as it was and we can always go back to it.\n3) **Nothing was lost or added:** every column's missing share matches the 1.2 audit. The conversion changed how missing values are written, not how many there are.\n\n**What we do next:** many columns are still stored as text (`object`) even though they hold numbers, dates or lap times. We fix that in 1.5.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.5 Semantic typing\n\nAfter 1.4 the missing values are real `NaN`s, but many columns are still stored as text (`object`) even though they hold dates, lap times or plain numbers. As long as they are text we cannot sort races by date, compute a driver's age, or compare lap times.\n\n\"Semantic\" typing means we convert each column according to what it means, not what it looks like. In 1.1 we saw that `time` means a lap time in `lap_times`, a time of day in `pit_stops`, and a total race time or a gap to the winner in `results`, so one rule cannot fit all of them.\n\nWe start by listing every column that is still stored as text.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for name, table in clean.items():\n    text_cols = list(table.select_dtypes(include=\"object\").columns)\n    print(name, \":\", text_cols)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.947072Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.947416Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.971992Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.947389Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.970982Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"circuits : ['circuitRef', 'name', 'location', 'country', 'url']\nconstructors : ['constructorRef', 'name', 'nationality', 'url']\nconstructor_results : ['status']\nconstructor_standings : ['positionText']\ndrivers : ['driverRef', 'number', 'code', 'forename', 'surname', 'dob', 'nationality', 'url']\ndriver_standings : ['positionText']\nraces : ['name', 'date', 'time', 'url', 'fp1_date', 'fp1_time', 'fp2_date', 'fp2_time', 'fp3_date', 'fp3_time', 'quali_date', 'quali_time', 'sprint_date', 'sprint_time']\nresults : ['number', 'position', 'positionText', 'time', 'milliseconds', 'fastestLap', 'rank', 'fastestLapTime', 'fastestLapSpeed']\nqualifying : ['q1', 'q2', 'q3']\nlap_times : ['time']\npit_stops : ['time', 'duration']\nsprint_results : ['position', 'positionText', 'time', 'milliseconds', 'fastestLap', 'fastestLapTime']\nseasons : ['url']\nstatus : ['status']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":40},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\nComparing this list with what we learned about each column in 1.1, the text columns fall into four kinds:\n\n1) **Real text, which stays text:** names, short names (`circuitRef`, `driverRef`), URLs, nationalities, driver codes, `positionText` and the `status` descriptions.\n2) **Dates stored as text:** `races.date` and `drivers.dob`. We convert both to real dates. We need race dates to put races in time order (1.1 showed that IDs are not in time order), and we need dates of birth if we use a driver's age as a feature.\n3) **Durations stored as text:** the qualifying times `q1`, `q2` and `q3`, the lap times in `lap_times.time`, the pit stop `duration`, and `fastestLapTime` in `results` and `sprint_results`. These are written as minutes:seconds (e.g. `1:38.109`), so `to_numeric` cannot read them. We convert them to seconds. `fastestLapTime` is post-race and never a feature, but it is exactly the brief's example of a lap-time string, so we convert it along with the others.\n4) **Numbers stored as text:** columns such as `results.position` and `results.milliseconds`. 1.1 suggested they were stored as text only because of the `\\N` placeholder. Now that 1.4 has turned those into `NaN`, they should convert directly.\n\n**Columns we leave as text on purpose:**\n- `results.time` and `sprint_results.time` mix the winner's total time with \"+gap\" values for everyone else, so one conversion cannot handle them. `milliseconds` already holds each driver's total time as a number.\n- `races.time` and `pit_stops.time` are times of day, not durations.\n- The session schedule columns in `races` (`fp1_date` to `sprint_time`) give the dates and times of practice, qualifying and sprint sessions. They are filled only for recent races and we do not use them.\n\nWe convert in that order: dates, then durations, then the remaining numbers.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"clean[\"races\"][\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(clean[\"races\"][\"date\"])\nclean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"] = pd.to_datetime(clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"])\n\nprint(\"Race dates:\", clean[\"races\"][\"date\"].min(), \"to\", clean[\"races\"][\"date\"].max(),\n      \"| missing:\", clean[\"races\"][\"date\"].isna().sum())\nprint(\"Dates of birth:\", clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"].min(), \"to\", clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"].max(),\n      \"| missing:\", clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"].isna().sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.973329Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.973667Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.989292Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.973639Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.988311Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Race dates: 1950-05-13 00:00:00 to 2024-12-08 00:00:00 | missing: 0\nDates of birth: 1896-12-28 00:00:00 to 2005-05-08 00:00:00 | missing: 0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":41},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Both columns are now real dates. `to_datetime` stops with an error if it meets a value it cannot read, so if this cell runs, every date was converted. The ranges are a sanity check: race dates should run from 1950 to 2024, and dates of birth should fall before that.\n\nNext come the durations. Before writing a conversion, we look at how they are written. 1.1 noted that pit stop `duration` looks like plain seconds but is stored as text, which means at least one value is written differently.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"duration = clean[\"pit_stops\"][\"duration\"]\nhas_minutes = duration.str.contains(\":\", na=False)\nprint(\"Pit stop durations written with minutes:\", has_minutes.sum(), \"of\", len(duration))\nprint(duration[has_minutes].head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.990759Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.991059Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.016339Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:39.991033Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.015401Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Pit stop durations written with minutes: 517 of 11371\n751     16:44.718\n2419     2:03.124\n2420     1:29.401\n2421     1:44.833\n2422     1:14.026\nName: duration, dtype: object\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":42},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"So durations come in two formats: `26.898` (seconds only) and `minutes:seconds` (e.g. `1:38.109`). The 517 pit stop durations written with minutes are stops that lasted a minute or more; the longest, `16:44.718`, is about 1,005 seconds. Pit stops are post-race information and never a feature, so we only note this here.\n\nOne small function can read both formats: split the text at every `:` and build up the total, multiplying by 60 each time we move to a smaller unit. `1:38.109` becomes 1 × 60 + 38.109 = 98.109 seconds, and `26.898` simply stays 26.898.\n\nThe function has no error handling on purpose. If a value has an unexpected format, the cell stops and we see it, instead of the value quietly turning into `NaN`. To prove nothing was lost, we count the missing values in each column before and after converting.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def to_seconds(text):\n    \"\"\"'1:38.109' -> 98.109 and '26.898' -> 26.898. Missing values stay missing.\"\"\"\n    if pd.isna(text):\n        return np.nan\n    seconds = 0\n    for part in text.split(\":\"):\n        seconds = seconds * 60 + float(part)\n    return seconds\n\nDURATIONS = [(\"qualifying\", \"q1\"), (\"qualifying\", \"q2\"), (\"qualifying\", \"q3\"),\n             (\"lap_times\", \"time\"), (\"pit_stops\", \"duration\"),\n             (\"results\", \"fastestLapTime\"), (\"sprint_results\", \"fastestLapTime\")]\n\nfor table, col in DURATIONS:\n    missing_before = clean[table][col].isna().sum()\n    clean[table][col] = clean[table][col].apply(to_seconds)\n    missing_after = clean[table][col].isna().sum()\n    print(f\"{table}.{col}: missing before {missing_before}, after {missing_after}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.017853Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.018361Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.816565Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.018324Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.815538Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"qualifying.q1: missing before 156, after 156\nqualifying.q2: missing before 4647, after 4647\nqualifying.q3: missing before 6865, after 6865\nlap_times.time: missing before 0, after 0\npit_stops.duration: missing before 0, after 0\nresults.fastestLapTime: missing before 18507, after 18507\nsprint_results.fastestLapTime: missing before 9, after 9\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":43},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The missing counts tell us no value was lost. They do not tell us the conversion is correct. For that, `lap_times` and `pit_stops` give us a built-in answer key: each row also has the same duration in `milliseconds`, recorded independently. If our seconds × 1000 match `milliseconds` on every row, the conversion is right.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for table, col in [(\"lap_times\", \"time\"), (\"pit_stops\", \"duration\")]:\n    t = clean[table]\n    diff = (t[col] * 1000 - t[\"milliseconds\"]).abs()\n    print(f\"{table}: largest difference from milliseconds = {diff.max():.3f} ms over {len(t)} rows\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.817563Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.817844Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.830477Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.817819Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.829463Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"lap_times: largest difference from milliseconds = 0.000 ms over 589081 rows\npit_stops: largest difference from milliseconds = 0.000 ms over 11371 rows\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":44},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Finally, the numbers stored as text. Instead of typing out a list of columns, we try converting every column that is still text. A column converts only if all of its values are numbers. If even one value is a word (such as \"HAM\" or \"R\"), `to_numeric` raises an error and the column stays text.\n\nWe deliberately do not use `errors=\"coerce\"`. It would turn any unexpected value into `NaN` without telling us, which is exactly the kind of silent data loss we are trying to avoid.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"converted = []\nfor name, table in clean.items():\n    for col in table.select_dtypes(include=\"object\").columns:\n        try:\n            table[col] = pd.to_numeric(table[col])\n            converted.append(f\"{name}.{col}\")\n        except (ValueError, TypeError):\n            pass  # contains words, so it is real text and stays text\n\nprint(\"Converted to numbers:\", len(converted))\nprint(converted)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.831546Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.832651Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.919348Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.832602Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.918447Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Converted to numbers: 10\n['drivers.number', 'results.number', 'results.position', 'results.milliseconds', 'results.fastestLap', 'results.rank', 'results.fastestLapSpeed', 'sprint_results.position', 'sprint_results.milliseconds', 'sprint_results.fastestLap']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":45},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Dates:** `races.date` (1950-05-13 to 2024-12-08) and `drivers.dob` (1896-12-28 to 2005-05-08) are real dates, with no value missing. Races can now be put in time order by `date`.\n2) **Durations:** the qualifying times, lap times, pit stop durations and fastest lap times are now in seconds, and no value was lost: every column has the same number of missing values before and after. In `q2` (4,647) and `q3` (6,865) these are the `\\N` and empty values from 1.2 added together (4,625 + 22 and 6,819 + 46). The lap times and pit stop durations match the independently recorded `milliseconds` on every row (largest difference 0.000 ms over 589,081 and 11,371 rows), so the conversion is correct. `fastestLapTime` has no such answer key, but it is written in the same `minutes:seconds` format that `lap_times` just showed the function reads correctly.\n3) **Numbers stored as text:** the 10 columns listed above are now numeric. They were text only because of `\\N`, which 1.4 removed.\n4) **What is still text:** real text (names, references, URLs, nationalities, driver codes, `positionText`, `status`) and the columns we left as text on purpose: `results.time` and `sprint_results.time` (totals mixed with gaps), the time-of-day columns, and the session schedule columns in `races`, which we do not use.\n\n**What we do next:** with the right types in place, we check the keys in 1.6: every ID column is unique and complete, and every ID that points to another table actually exists there.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.6 Key audit\n\nThe tables only work together if their IDs are reliable. The brief asks for two checks:\n\n1) **Primary keys are unique and non-null.** A primary key is the column (or columns) that identifies one row of a table, such as `raceId` in `races`. If an ID were repeated, a join on it would silently copy rows; if it were missing, the row could not be linked to anything.\n2) **Foreign keys reconcile.** A foreign key is a column that points to a primary key in another table, such as `results.raceId` pointing to `races`. Every value must exist in the table it points to. An ID with nothing behind it (an \"orphan\") would lose its race date, driver or team when we join.\n\n**Choosing the primary keys.** Twelve tables have their own ID column, which is the natural primary key. `lap_times` and `pit_stops` have none, so we use the combination that 1.1 showed identifies one row: one lap of one driver in one race (`raceId, driverId, lap`) and one stop of one driver in one race (`raceId, driverId, stop`).\n\n**Join keys.** Later sections join tables on columns that are not primary keys, and those joins assume one row per key. We check them here too:\n- `races` on (`year`, `round`), which we use to put races in order.\n- `qualifying`, `driver_standings` and `constructor_standings` on race + driver (or race + constructor), which we attach to each result.\n- `results` on (`raceId`, `driverId`), which is our unit of analysis.\n\nFrom plot 5 in 1.3 we expect `results` to fail this last check because of shared drives. That is a problem with the table's grain (what one row represents), not a broken key, since the primary key of `results` is `resultId`. We handle it in 1.7.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def check_key(table, key):\n    \"\"\"One row of the audit: how many rows have a missing key value, and how many repeat a key.\"\"\"\n    t = clean[table]\n    n_null = t[key].isna().any(axis=1).sum()\n    n_dup = t.duplicated(key).sum()\n    return {\"table\": table, \"key\": \", \".join(key), \"rows\": len(t),\n            \"null keys\": n_null, \"duplicate keys\": n_dup, \"pass\": n_null == 0 and n_dup == 0}\n\nPRIMARY_KEYS = [(\"circuits\", [\"circuitId\"]), (\"constructors\", [\"constructorId\"]),\n                (\"drivers\", [\"driverId\"]), (\"seasons\", [\"year\"]), (\"status\", [\"statusId\"]),\n                (\"races\", [\"raceId\"]), (\"qualifying\", [\"qualifyId\"]),\n                (\"sprint_results\", [\"resultId\"]), (\"results\", [\"resultId\"]),\n                (\"constructor_results\", [\"constructorResultsId\"]),\n                (\"driver_standings\", [\"driverStandingsId\"]),\n                (\"constructor_standings\", [\"constructorStandingsId\"]),\n                (\"lap_times\", [\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"lap\"]),\n                (\"pit_stops\", [\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"stop\"])]\n\nJOIN_KEYS = [(\"races\", [\"year\", \"round\"]),\n             (\"qualifying\", [\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]),\n             (\"driver_standings\", [\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]),\n             (\"constructor_standings\", [\"raceId\", \"constructorId\"]),\n             (\"results\", [\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])]\n\npk_check = pd.DataFrame([check_key(t, k) for t, k in PRIMARY_KEYS])\njoin_check = pd.DataFrame([check_key(t, k) for t, k in JOIN_KEYS])\n\nprint(\"Primary keys:\", pk_check[\"pass\"].sum(), \"of\", len(pk_check), \"pass\")\ndisplay(pk_check)\nprint(\"Join keys:\", join_check[\"pass\"].sum(), \"of\", len(join_check), \"pass\")\ndisplay(join_check)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.920486Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:40.920773Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T