21</th>\n      <td>pit_stops.raceId → races.raceId</td>\n      <td>11371</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>True</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>22</th>\n      <td>pit_stops.driverId → drivers.driverId</td>\n      <td>11371</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>True</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":47},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Every primary key is unique and complete:** all 14 tables pass, including the two combined keys of `lap_times` and `pit_stops`. Every row can be identified, and a join on these IDs will not copy rows.\n2) **Every foreign key reconciles:** all 23 links pass, with no missing values and no orphans. Every result, qualifying entry, standing, lap and pit stop points to a race, driver, team and status that exist, so joins on these IDs will not lose information.\n3) **The join keys we rely on later are safe, except one:**\n   - `races` has one race per (`year`, `round`), so ordering races by season and round is well defined.\n   - `qualifying`, `driver_standings` and `constructor_standings` have one row per driver (or team) per race, so attaching them to results will not create extra rows.\n   - `results` fails on (`raceId`, `driverId`) with 91 repeated keys. This is the shared-drive problem from plot 5: 176 rows form 85 pairs, and 176 − 85 = 91 rows are extra copies. It is not a broken key, since `resultId` is unique, but it breaks our unit of analysis.\n\n**What we do next:** in 1.7 we look at these 85 pairs and choose which row to keep for each driver in each race.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.7 One row per driver per race\n\nOur modeling table needs exactly one row per (`raceId`, `driverId`), because each row is one prediction. 1.6 found 91 extra rows in `results`, spread over 85 driver-race pairs. The brief and the F1 introduction explain where they come from: until 1957 a team could hand a car to another driver mid-race, so a driver who drove two cars in one race has two result rows, one per car.\n\nThe brief asks us to choose a rule for which row to keep. Before choosing, we look at what these rows contain. The rule must use only information known before the race, so we do not want to pick a row by its result.\n\n**Did the driver start this car?** `grid` is a pre-race value, and each grid slot belongs to one car. If two drivers in the same race have the same non-zero grid, they shared that car and only one of them started it. So for every duplicated row we count how many drivers in that race have the same grid position.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# results with their season and race name (results -> races through raceId)\nresults = clean[\"results\"].merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"name\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\ndup = results[results.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], keep=False)].copy()\n\n# how many different drivers have each non-zero grid slot in each race\ndrivers_per_slot = (results[results[\"grid\"] > 0]\n                    .groupby([\"raceId\", \"grid\"])[\"driverId\"].nunique()\n                    .rename(\"drivers_on_slot\").reset_index())\ndup = dup.merge(drivers_per_slot, on=[\"raceId\", \"grid\"], how=\"left\")\ndup[\"drivers_on_slot\"] = dup[\"drivers_on_slot\"].fillna(0).astype(int)  # grid 0 has no slot\n\n# label each row: grid 0, a slot only this driver has, or a slot shared with another driver\ndup[\"row_type\"] = np.select([dup[\"grid\"] == 0, dup[\"drivers_on_slot\"] == 1],\n                            [\"grid 0\", \"own slot\"], default=\"shared slot\")\n\npairs = dup.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])\nprint(\"Rows:\", len(dup), \"| pairs:\", pairs.ngroups, \"| seasons:\", dup[\"year\"].min(), \"-\", dup[\"year\"].max())\nprint(\"\\nRows per pair:\", pairs.size().value_counts().to_dict())\nprint(\"\\nRow types:\")\nprint(dup[\"row_type\"].value_counts())\nprint(\"\\n'Own slot' rows per pair:\")\nprint(pairs[\"row_type\"].apply(lambda s: (s == \"own slot\").sum()).value_counts().sort_index())\n\ndisplay(dup.sort_values([\"year\", \"raceId\", \"driverId\"])\n           [[\"year\", \"name\", \"driverId\", \"constructorId\", \"number\", \"grid\", \"drivers_on_slot\",\n             \"row_type\", \"positionOrder\", \"positionText\", \"points\"]].head(12))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.079354Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.079631Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.145085Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.079606Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.144185Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows: 176 | pairs: 85 | seasons: 1950 - 1978\n\nRows per pair: {2: 79, 3: 6}\n\nRow types:\nrow_type\nshared slot    115\nown slot        55\ngrid 0           6\nName: count, dtype: int64\n\n'Own slot' rows per pair:\nrow_type\n0    31\n1    53\n2     1\nName: count, dtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"    year                name  driverId  constructorId  number  grid  \\\n84  1950    Indianapolis 500       529            150    14.0     8   \n88  1950    Indianapolis 500       529            113    17.0     9   \n85  1950   French Grand Prix       627            154    20.0     6   \n89  1950   French Grand Prix       627            154    26.0    15   \n86  1950  Italian Grand Prix       579             51    18.0     1   \n91  1950  Italian Grand Prix       579             51    60.0     7   \n87  1950  Italian Grand Prix       647              6    16.0     2   \n90  1950  Italian Grand Prix       647              6    48.0     6   \n81  1951   French Grand Prix       579             51    24.0     1   \n92  1951   French Grand Prix       579             51     8.0     7   \n80  1951   French Grand Prix       647              6    14.0     6   \n82  1951   French Grand Prix       647              6    12.0  