d>2</td>\n      <td>shared slot</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>92</th>\n      <td>1951</td>\n      <td>French Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>579</td>\n      <td>51</td>\n      <td>8.0</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>shared slot</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>5.0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>80</th>\n      <td>1951</td>\n      <td>French Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>647</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>14.0</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>shared slot</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>82</th>\n      <td>1951</td>\n      <td>French Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>647</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>12.0</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>own slot</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>R</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":48},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Shared drives last longer than the 1950s:** the 176 rows form 85 pairs and run from 1950 to 1978, while the brief describes them as \"mainly the 1950s\". 79 pairs have two rows and 6 have three, meaning the driver appears in three different cars in the same race.\n2) **Most duplicated rows sit on a shared grid slot:** 115 of the 176 rows have a grid position that another driver in the same race also has, 55 have a slot of their own, and 6 have grid 0. A grid slot belongs to one car, so a slot with two drivers is one car driven by two people. This confirms the shared-drive explanation.\n3) **For 53 of the 85 pairs, the grid tells us which car the driver started in:** exactly one of their rows is on a slot nobody else has. For example, driver 647 at the 1950 Italian Grand Prix has a row on grid 2 that is his alone (retired, ranked 17th) and a row on grid 6 that he shares with another driver (finished 2nd). He started from grid 2 and later took over the car that started 6th.\n4) **For the other 32 pairs, the grid alone does not decide:** 31 pairs have no row on a slot of their own, and 1 has two. Driver 579 at the 1951 French Grand Prix is an example: both of his rows are on shared slots (grid 1, ranked 11th; grid 7, won), so two drivers swapped cars and each car appears under both of them.\n5) **Choosing by the result would pick the wrong car:** in both examples the car the driver took over finished better than the car he started in. Keeping the row with the best `positionOrder` would keep the grid 6 and grid 7 rows, attaching a podium to a grid position the driver never started from. It would also choose the row by the race outcome, which is post-race information. We rule this option out.\n\n**Can one pre-race rule cover all 85 pairs?** In both examples the car the driver started in has the better (lower) grid: a leading driver whose car failed took over a teammate's car that started further back. If this holds in general, \"keep the row with the lowest non-zero grid\" picks the car the driver started in for every pair. We test it on the 53 pairs where we already know the answer.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"dup[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] = dup.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])[\"row_type\"].transform(lambda s: (s == \"own slot\").sum())\n\n# for each pair, the row with the lowest non-zero grid\non_grid = dup[dup[\"grid\"] > 0]\nlowest = on_grid.loc[on_grid.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])[\"grid\"].idxmin()]\n\nprint(\"Pairs with no row on a non-zero grid:\", pairs.ngroups - on_grid.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]).ngroups)\nprint(\"Pairs where two rows share the lowest grid:\",\n      (on_grid.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])[\"grid\"].apply(lambda g: (g == g.min()).sum()) > 1).sum())\n\n# the test: in the 53 clear pairs, is the lowest-grid row the own-slot row?\nclear = lowest[lowest[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] == 1]\nprint(\"\\nClear pairs:\", len(clear), \"| lowest grid picks the own-slot row:\", (clear[\"row_type\"] == \"own slot\").sum())\n\n# the pairs the grid alone could not decide, with the row the rule would keep\nunclear = dup[dup[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] != 1].copy()\nunclear[\"kept\"] = unclear.index.isin(lowest.index)\ndisplay(unclear.sort_values([\"year\", \"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"grid\"])\n               [[\"year\", \"name\", \"driverId\", \"number\", \"grid\", \"drivers_on_slot\", \"row_type\",\n                 \"kept\", \"positionOrder\", \"positionText\"]].head(20))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.146286Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.147541Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.203214Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.147511Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.202282Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Pairs with no row on a non-zero grid: 3\nPairs where two rows share the lowest grid: 1\n\nClear pairs: 53 | lowest grid picks the own-slot row: 42\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"     year               name  driverId  number  grid  drivers_on_slot  \\\n81   1951  French Grand Prix       579    24.0     1                2   \n92   1951  French Grand Prix       579     8.0     7                2   \n93   1951  French Grand Prix       786    24.0     1                2   \n79   1951  French Grand Prix       786     8.0     7                2   \n62   1953   Indianapolis 500       509     2.0    25                2   \n103  1953   Indianapolis 500       509    49.0    28                2   \n110  1953  German Grand Prix       633     1.0     1                2   \n73   1953  German Grand Prix       633     4.0     6                2   \n72   1953  German Grand Prix       647     1.0     1                2   \n111  1953  German Grand Prix       647     4.0     6                2   \n74   1953   Swiss Grand Prix       579    32.0     1                2   \n113  1953   Swiss 