ahead of him, so the pattern from our two examples does not hold in general.\n2) **Some pairs have no usable grid at all:** in 3 pairs every row has grid 0, and in 1 pair two rows share the lowest grid, so the rule could not even make a choice there.\n\n**Our rule**\n\n1) **If exactly one of a driver's rows is on a grid slot nobody else has, we keep that row.** Only this driver is linked to that car, so it is the car they started in. This decides 53 of the 85 pairs using only `grid`, which is known before the race.\n2) **Otherwise, we remove the driver's rows for that race.** For the remaining 32 pairs, nothing known before the race tells us which car the driver started in. Keeping the lowest grid would give about one in five of them a starting position they never had (point 1), and `grid` is our main feature. A wrong grid next to the label does more harm than a missing row from the 1950s–1970s.\n\n**Rejected rule:** keeping the row with the best `positionOrder`. It chooses by the race result, which is post-race information, and 1.7 showed it attaches a podium to a car the driver did not start in.\n\nThe rule is applied to `clean[\"results\"]`, so every later step uses one row per driver per race. We check the row and podium counts before and after, and that no duplicates are left.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# from the duplicated pairs, keep only the own-slot row of each clear pair\nkept_ids = dup.loc[(dup[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] == 1) & (dup[\"row_type\"] == \"own slot\"), \"resultId\"]\ndrop_ids = dup.loc[~dup[\"resultId\"].isin(kept_ids), \"resultId\"]\n\nrows_before = len(clean[\"results\"])\npodiums_before = (clean[\"results\"][\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).sum()\n\nclean[\"results\"] = clean[\"results\"][~clean[\"results\"][\"resultId\"].isin(drop_ids)].copy()\n\nprint(\"Rows: before\", rows_before, \"| after\", len(clean[\"results\"]), \"| removed\", rows_before - len(clean[\"results\"]))\nprint(\"Pairs kept with their starting car:\", len(kept_ids),\n      \"| pairs removed:\", unclear[[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]].drop_duplicates().shape[0])\nprint(\"Podium rows: before\", podiums_before, \"| after\", (clean[\"results\"][\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).sum())\nprint(\"Duplicated (raceId, driverId) left:\", clean[\"results\"].duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]).sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.204647Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.204935Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.229373Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.204905Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.228337Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows: before 26759 | after 26636 | removed 123\nPairs kept with their starting car: 53 | pairs removed: 32\nPodium rows: before 3397 | after 3373\nDuplicated (raceId, driverId) left: 0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":50},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **The grain is fixed:** no (`raceId`, `driverId`) pair appears more than once, so every row of `results` is now one driver in one race, which is our unit of analysis.\n2) **Exactly the rows we intended were removed:** 26,759 → 26,636 rows, 123 fewer. That is the 176 duplicated rows minus the 53 we kept, one per clear pair. The other 32 pairs were removed completely.\n3) **The removed rows were unusually successful:** 24 of the 123 removed rows were podiums (20%), against 13% (3,397 of 26,759) in `results` as a whole. This fits what we saw above: drivers often took over a car that was running well, so these rows carry many good results. Keeping them would have attached those results to a starting position the driver did not have.\n4) **The cost is small and limited to early seasons:** all removed rows are from 1950–1978, under 0.5% of `results`.\n\n**What we do next:** the table has one row per driver per race, but not every row is a driver who started the race. In 1.8 we decide which rows belong in the analysis.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**A second form of shared drive.** The F1 introduction describes shared drives in two ways: the same driver with two result rows, which we handled above, or two drivers attached to one car's result. The second form does not show up in the (`raceId`, `driverId`) check, because the two drivers have different IDs.\n\nA car finishes a race only once, so in the data this form would look like two drivers with the same finishing place (`positionOrder`) in the same race. We look for such rows, and check whether both drivers also have the same grid, laps and status, which would mean they are one car.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# two different drivers with the same finishing place in the same race\nr = clean[\"results\"].merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"name\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\nsame_car = r[r.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"positionOrder\"], keep=False)]\ncars = same_car.groupby([\"raceId\", \"positionOrder\"])\n\nprint(\"Rows:\", len(same_car), \"| cars:\", cars.ngroups, \"| races:\", same_car[\"raceId\"].nunique(),\n      \"| seasons:\", same_car[\"year\"].min(), \"-\", same_car[\"year\"].max())\nfor col in [\"grid\", \"laps\", \"statusId\"]:\n    print(f\"Same {col} for both drivers in every car:\", (cars[col].nunique() == 1).all())\nprint(\"Podium rows:\", (same_car[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).sum())\n\ndisplay(same_car.sort_values([\"year\", \"raceId\", \"positionOrder\"])\n                [[\"year\", \"name\", \"driverId\", \"grid\", \"laps\", \"positionOrder\", \"positionText\"]].head(6))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.230689Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.23099Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.263358Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.230963Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.262604Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows: 56 | cars: 28 | races: 20 | seasons: 1950 - 1957\nSame grid for both drivers in every car: True\nSame laps for both drivers in every car: True\nSame statusId for both drivers in every car: True\nPodium rows: 4\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"       year                name  driverId  grid  laps  positionOrder  \\\n19998  1950  British Grand Prix       778    20    64             10   \n20141  1950  British Grand Prix       788    20    64             10   \n20008  1950  British Grand Prix       661    10     5             20   \n20142  1950  British Grand Prix       666    10     5             20   \n20057  1950    Indianapolis 500       798    17   108             27   \n20143  1950    Indianapolis 500       803    17   108             27   \n\n      positionText  \n19998           10  \n20141           10  \n20008            R  \n20142            R  \n20057            R  \n20143            R  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>year</th>\n      <th>name</th>\n      <th>driverId</th>\n      <th>grid</th>\n      <th>laps</th>\n      <th>positionOrder</th>\n      <th>positionText</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>19998</th>\n      <td>1950</td>\n      <td>British Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>778</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>64</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>20141</th>\n      <td>1950</td>\n      <td>British Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>788</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>64</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>20008</th>\n      <td>1950</td>\n      <td>British Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>661</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>5</td>