r\", len(clean[\"results\"]), \"| removed\", rows_before - len(clean[\"results\"]))\nprint(\"Same finishing place twice in one race, left:\", clean[\"results\"].duplicated([\"raceId\", \"positionOrder\"]).sum())\nprint(\"Duplicated (raceId, driverId) left:\", clean[\"results\"].duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]).sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.26427Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.264507Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.280642Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.264486Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.279686Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows: before 26636 | after 26580 | removed 56\nSame finishing place twice in one race, left: 0\nDuplicated (raceId, driverId) left: 0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":52},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **56 rows removed:** 26,636 → 26,580, exactly the two rows of each of the 28 cars.\n2) **Every car now appears once per race:** no finishing place appears twice in the same race, and still no driver appears twice.\n\n**What we do next:** the table has one row per driver per race, but not every row is a driver who started the race. In 1.8 we decide which rows belong in the analysis.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1.8 Scope filters and label fixes\n\nSome rows in `results` are not predictions we would ever make. The F1 introduction asks us to decide whether drivers who did not (pre-)qualify belong in the modeling table, and 1.1 raised three more issues. This step handles all of them:\n\n1) **Drivers who never started:** `positionText` F (failed to qualify) and W (withdrawn). At our cutoff, after qualifying and before the start, we already know these drivers will not race, so there is nothing to predict. Before removing them we check two things: that F and W agree with the `status` column, and what the 9 E (excluded) rows are, since 1.1 left them open.\n2) **The Indianapolis 500 (1950–1960):** the F1 introduction describes it as a separate American race that almost no F1 regulars entered, which distorts the home-race question. 1.1 showed that the same circuit also hosted 8 real US Grands Prix (2000–2007), so we remove it by race name, not by circuit.\n3) **Two labels written in two ways:** `United States` → `USA` in `circuits.country`, and `Argentinian ` (with a trailing space) → `Argentine` in `drivers.nationality`. Both matter for Question 2, which matches drivers to their home circuits.\n\nFirst, the non-starters.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# results with the reason each race ended (results -> status through statusId)\nres_status = clean[\"results\"].merge(clean[\"status\"], on=\"statusId\", how=\"left\")\n\nletters = res_status[res_status[\"positionText\"].isin([\"F\", \"W\", \"E\"])]\nprint(\"Status of the F, W and E rows:\")\nprint(pd.crosstab(letters[\"status\"], letters[\"positionText\"]))\n\nnon_start_status = res_status[res_status[\"status\"].isin([\"Did not qualify\", \"Did not prequalify\", \"Withdrew\"])]\nprint(\"\\npositionText of rows whose status says they never started:\")\nprint(pd.crosstab(non_start_status[\"status\"], non_start_status[\"positionText\"]))\n\nprint(\"\\nPer letter: rows with grid > 0, rows with laps > 0, podium rows\")\nprint(letters.groupby(\"positionText\").agg(rows=(\"grid\", \"size\"),\n                                          grid_above_0=(\"grid\", lambda g: (g > 0).sum()),\n                                          laps_above_0=(\"laps\", lambda l: (l > 0).sum()),\n                                          podiums=(\"positionOrder\", lambda p: (p <= 3).sum())))\n\ndisplay(letters[letters[\"positionText\"] == \"E\"]\n        .merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"name\"]], on=\"raceId\")\n        [[\"year\", \"name\", \"driverId\", \"grid\", \"laps\", \"status\", \"positionOrder\"]])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"