  </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>1992</td>\n      <td>German Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>135</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>32</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>1988</td>\n      <td>San Marino Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>78</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>27</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>1988</td>\n      <td>Monaco Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>131</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>31</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>1988</td>\n      <td>Mexican Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>131</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>31</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>5</th>\n      <td>1988</td>\n      <td>French Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>160</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>31</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>6</th>\n      <td>1987</td>\n      <td>Monaco Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>157</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>26</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>7</th>\n      <td>1987</td>\n      <td>British Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>160</td>\n      <td>19</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>26</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>8</th>\n      <td>2015</td>\n      <td>Brazilian Grand Prix</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>8</td>\n      <td>70</td>\n      <td>Excluded</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":53},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **F means the driver did not get a grid slot:** all 1,364 F rows have a qualifying reason as status: `Did not qualify` (1,022), `Did not prequalify` (330), `107% Rule` (9) and `Injury` (3). In the other direction, every `Did not qualify` and `Did not prequalify` row is an F. The letter code and the status agree.\n2) **W means the driver did not take the start, for many reasons:** only 226 of the 334 W rows have the status `Withdrew`; the rest give the cause, such as `Accident` (29), `Engine` (19), `Gearbox` (8) or an injury (14). 133 W rows have a grid position, so these drivers qualified and then could not start. On the other side, 20 rows with status `Withdrew` have a finishing position or `R`, so they did start and stopped during the race. The letter code, not the status text, tells us whether a driver started.\n3) **E is mixed:** 8 of the 9 excluded rows completed no laps, so these drivers were excluded before the race. The 2015 Brazilian Grand Prix row is different: the driver started from grid 8, completed 70 laps, and was excluded after the race. That is a race we would have predicted, with a non-podium label, so it stays.\n4) **None of these rows is a podium:** removing them takes away no positive labels, only rows whose outcome was settled before the start.\n5) **A few rows do not fit:** 1 F row and 5 W rows show laps above 0. None of them is a podium, so we follow the letter code for them too.\n\n**Our rule:** remove every F and W row, and the E rows with no laps completed. `laps` is post-race information, but here it only decides which rows are in scope, never a feature, and only within the E rows, where the letter alone cannot tell us whether the driver started.\n\n**A limitation we accept:** 133 W drivers had a grid slot, so some withdrawals were only known very close to the start, possibly after our cutoff. They are a small group of certain non-podiums, so removing them barely changes the table.\n\nNext we apply all three changes and track the row count and podium share after each one.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def podium_share(table):\n    return round(100 * (table[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).mean(), 2)\n\nsteps = [{\"step\": \"after 1.7\", \"rows\": len(clean[\"results\"]), \"podium %\": podium_share(clean[\"results\"])}]\n\n# 1) drivers who never started: F and W, and E rows with no laps\nr = clean[\"results\"]\nnever_started = r[\"positionText\"].isin([\"F\", \"W\"]) | ((r[\"positionText\"] == \"E\") & (r[\"laps\"] == 0))\nclean[\"results\"] = r[~never_started].copy()\nsteps.append({\"step\": \"without non-starters\", \"rows\": len(clean[\"results\"]), \"podium %\": podium_share(clean[\"results\"])})\n\n# 2) the Indianapolis 500, selected by race name (not by circuit)\nindy = clean[\"races\"][clean[\"races\"][\"name\"] == \"Indianapolis 500\"]\nprint(\"Indianapolis 500 races:\", len(indy), \"| seasons:\", indy[\"year\"].min(), \"-\", indy[\"year\"].max())\nclean[\"results\"] = clean[\"results\"][~clean[\"results\"][\"raceId\"].isin(indy[\"raceId\"])].copy()\nsteps.append({\"step\": \"without Indianapolis 500\", \"rows\": len(clean[\"results\"]), \"podium %\": podium_share(clean[\"results\"])})\n\nsteps = pd.DataFrame(steps)\nsteps[\"removed\"] = -steps[\"rows\"].diff()\ndisplay(steps)\n\n# 3) one label per country and nationality\nprint(\"Before:\", (clean[\"circuits\"][\"country\"] == \"United States\").sum(), \"'United States' circuits |\",\n      (clean[\"drivers\"][\"nationality\"] == \"Argentinian \").sum(), \"'Argentinian ' drivers\")\nclean[\"circuits\"][\"country\"] = clean[\"circuits\"][\"country\"].replace({\"United States\": \"USA\"})\nclean[\"drivers\"][\"nationality\"] = clean[\"drivers\"][\"nationality\"].replace({\"Argentinian \": \"Argentine\"})\nprint(\"After: \", (clean[\"circuits\"][\"country\"] == \"USA\").sum(), \"'USA' circuits |\",\n      (clean[\"drivers\"][\"nationality\"] == \"Argentine\").sum(), \"'Argentine' drivers |\",\n      clean[\"drivers\"][\"nationality\"].nunique(), \"nationality values\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.342629Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.343274Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.380139Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.343235Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.379095Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Indianapolis 500 races: 11 | seasons: 1950 - 1960\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                       step   rows  podium %  removed\n0                 after 1.7  26580     12.67      NaN\n1      without non-starters  24874     13.54   1706.0\n2  without Indianapolis 500  24524     13.61    350.0","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>step</th>\n      <th>rows</th>\n      <th>podium %</th>\n      <th>removed</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>after 1.7</td>\n      <td>26580</td>\n      <td>12.67</td>\n      <td>NaN</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>without non-starters</td>\n      <td>24874</td>\n      <td>13.54</td>\n      <td>1706.0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>without Indianapolis 500</td>\n      <td>24524</td>\n      <td>13.61</td>\n      <td>350.0</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Before: 1 'United States' circuits | 1 'Argentinian ' drivers\nAfter:  12 'USA' circuits | 25 'Argentine' drivers | 42 nationality values\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":54},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Non-starters were the largest group of rows outside our scope:** removing them took out 1,706 rows (1,364 F, 334 W and 8 E), 6.4% of the table. Since none of them was a podium, the podium share rose from 12.66% to 13.53%. The rows that are left are drivers who actually raced, which is what we predict.\n2) **The Indianapolis 500 is gone:** 11 races from 1950 to 1960 and 372 rows, about 34 entrants per race, far more than a typical Grand Prix of that time. The podium share moved only slightly (13.53% → 13.59%). Its races stay in `races`, so the foreign keys from 1.6 still reconcile.\n3) **Each country and nationality now has one label:** all 12 US circuits are labelled `USA`, and the 25 Argentine drivers share one label, leaving 42 nationality values instead of 43. Question 2 can now match drivers to circuits without missing anyone because of spelling.\n\n**What we do next:** Part B is complete. In Part C we repeat the EDA from Part A on the cleaned data to see what the cleaning changed.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Part C — EDA after cleaning\n\n## 1.9 What did the cleaning change?\n\nThe brief asks for EDA both before and after cleaning, to show the impact of the cleaning. We repeat three views from Part A on the cleaned data and put them next to the raw versions:\n\n1) **Missing values:** the audit from 1.2, run on `clean`, compared column by column with the raw audit.\n2) **Rows removed:** how many rows each cleaning step took out of `results`.\n3) **The season views from 1.3:** entrants per race, podium share and grid = 0, before and after.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"audit_clean = audit(clean)\nprint(\"Placeholders left in clean:\", audit_clean[\"n_sentinel\"].sum() + audit_clean[\"n_empty\"].sum())\n\ncompare = audit_before.merge(audit_clean, on=[\"table\", \"column\"], suffixes=(\"_raw\", \"_clean\"))\nchanged = compare[compare[\"pct_missing_raw\"] != compare[\"pct_missing_clean\"]]\nprint(\"Columns whose missing share changed:\", len(changed), \"of\", len(compare))\ndisplay(changed[[\"table\", \"column\", \"pct_missing_raw\", \"pct_missing_clean\"]])\n\nlabels = changed[\"table\"] + \".\" + changed[\"column\"]\ny = np.arange(len(changed))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 0.5 * len(changed) + 1))\nax.barh(y - 0.2, changed[\"pct_missing_raw\"], height=0.4, label=\"raw\")\nax.barh(y + 0.2, changed[\"pct_missing_clean\"], height=0.4, label=\"clean\")\nax.set_yticks(y)\nax.set_yticklabels(labels)\nax.invert_yaxis()\nax.set_xlabel(\"% of rows missing\")\nax.set_title(\"Missing share per column, raw vs clean (columns that changed)\")\nax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.381592Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.381974Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.674433Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.381936Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.673551Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Placeholders left in clean: 0\nColumns whose missing share changed: 7 of 120\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"      table           column  pct_missing_raw  pct_missing_clean\n66  results         position             40.9               36.8\n71  results             time             71.3               69.2\n72  results     milliseconds             71.3               69.2\n73  results       fastestLap             69.2               66.4\n74  results             rank             68.2               65.5\n75  results   fastestLapTime             69.2               66.4\n76  results  fastestLapSpeed             69.2               66.4","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>table</th>\n      <th>column</th>\n      <th>pct_missing_raw</th>\n      <th>pct_missing_clean</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>66</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>position</td>\n      <td>40.9</td>\n      <td>36.8</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>71</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>time</td>\n      <td>71.3</td>\n      <td>69.2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>72</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>milliseconds</td>\n      <td>71.3</td>\n      <td>69.2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>73</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>fastestLap</td>\n      <td>69.2</td>\n      <td>66.4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>74</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>rank</td>\n      <td>68.2</td>\n      <td>65.5</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>75</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>fastestLapTime</td>\n      <td>69.2</td>\n      <td>66.4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>76</th>\n      <td>results</td>\n      <td>fastestLapSpeed</td>\n      <td>69.2</td>\n      <td>66.4</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 800x450 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":55},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **No placeholders are left:** the audit finds no `\\N` or empty strings in any table of `clean`.\n2) **Only 7 of 120 columns changed, all in `results`:** the cleaning removed rows only from `results`. Every other table keeps exactly the missing share it had in 1.2, including `qualifying`, whose coverage gaps are a property of the data, not of our cleaning.\n3) **Every change is a drop:** the removed rows were mostly drivers who never raced, so they had no finishing position, race time or fastest lap. `position` fell from 40.9% to 36.8% missing, and the time and fastest-lap columns by 2–3 points each.\n4) **What is still missing does not affect us:** `position` is still missing for 36.8% of rows, because retired drivers are not classified, which is why our target uses the always-filled `positionOrder`. The time and fastest-lap columns are post-race and are never features.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Where did the rows go?** Each cleaning step that removed rows from `results`, and how many it removed.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"removed = pd.Series({\"shared drives (1.7)\": len(raw[\"results\"]) - steps.loc[0, \"rows\"],\n                     \"non-starters (1.8)\": steps.loc[1, \"removed\"],\n                     \"Indianapolis 500 (1.8)\": steps.loc[2, \"removed\"]})\n\nax = removed.plot(kind=\"barh\", figsize=(8, 3))\nax.invert_yaxis()\nfor i, v in enumerate(removed):\n    ax.text(v, i, f\" {int(v):,}\", va=\"center\")\nax.set_xlabel(\"rows removed from results\")\nax.set_title(\"Rows removed by each cleaning step\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"results: raw\", len(raw[\"results\"]), \"| clean\", len(clean[\"results\"]),\n      f\"| kept {100 * len(clean['results']) / len(raw['results']):.1f}%\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.675586Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.675879Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.838489Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.675853Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.837329Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 800x300 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"results: raw 26759 | clean 24524 | kept 91.6%\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":56},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Non-starters were by far the largest removal:** 1,706 rows, against 372 for the Indianapolis 500 and 123 for shared drives.\n2) **Most of the table is kept:** 24,558 of 26,759 rows (91.8%) remain. Each removal has a documented reason: a driver who never started (1.8), a race outside the scope of the question (1.8), or a second row for the same driver in the same race (1.7).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Did the season picture change?** We redraw the views from 1.3 on the cleaned `results`: entrants per race, podium share, and the share of rows with grid = 0.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"res_clean = clean[\"results\"].merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\nres_clean[\"podium\"] = res_clean[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3\n\nper_season_clean = res_clean.groupby(\"year\").agg(races=(\"raceId\", \"nunique\"),\n                                                 rows=(\"raceId\", \"size\"),\n                                                 podium_rows=(\"podium\", \"sum\"))\nper_season_clean[\"entrants_per_race\"] = per_season_clean[\"rows\"] / per_season_clean[\"races\"]\nper_season_clean[\"podium_share\"] = 100 * per_season_clean[\"podium_rows\"] / per_season_clean[\"rows\"]\ngrid_zero_clean = 100 * res_clean.groupby(\"year\")[\"grid\"].apply(lambda g: (g == 0).mean())\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(18, 4))\nper_season[\"entrants_per_race\"].plot(ax=axes[0], label=\"raw\")\nper_season_clean[\"entrants_per_race\"].plot(ax=axes[0], label=\"clean\")\naxes[0].axhline(20, linestyle=\"--\", color=\"grey\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Average entrants per race\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"entrants\")\n\nper_season[\"podium_share\"].plot(ax=axes[1], label=\"raw\")\nper_season_clean[\"podium_share\"].plot(ax=axes[1], label=\"clean\")\naxes[1].axhline(15, linestyle=\"--\", color=\"grey\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"Share of result rows that are podiums\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"%\")\n\ngrid_zero_share.sum(axis=1).plot(ax=axes[2], label=\"raw\")\ngrid_zero_clean.plot(ax=axes[2], label=\"clean\")\naxes[2].set_title(\"Share of rows with grid = 0\")\naxes[2].set_ylabel(\"% of result rows\")\n\nfor ax in axes:\n    ax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\ngrid0 = res_clean[res_clean[\"grid\"] == 0]\nprint(\"Rows with grid = 0 after cleaning:\", len(grid0), \"of\", len(res_clean),\n      \"| podiums among them:\", grid0[\"podium\"].sum(),\n      \"| seasons:\", grid0[\"year\"].min(), \"-\", grid0[\"year\"].max())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.839707Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.84142Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.442423Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:41.841369Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.441288Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1800x400 with 3 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows with grid = 0 after cleaning: 80 of 24524 | podiums among them: 0 | seasons: 1981 - 2024\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":57},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **The late-1980s peak in field size came from non-starters:** in the raw data the average number of entrants per race climbs to almost 39 around 1989. After cleaning it stays around 25–26, because most of those entrants failed to (pre-)qualify. From the late 1990s the two lines are almost identical, since modern seasons have hardly any non-starters.\n2) **The podium share is much more stable, but not constant:** the raw dip to about 8% around 1989 is gone. After cleaning the share stays between about 11.5% and 19%, and from around 2010 it sits close to the expected 15% (3 of 20). The remaining variation follows real field sizes: more starters means a lower share. The base rate of a podium therefore depends on the era, which matters for the data split and is a limitation we report.\n3) **Almost all grid = 0 rows were non-starters:** the raw peaks above 20% in the early 1960s and above 30% around 1989 disappear completely. Only 80 of 24,558 rows still have grid 0, all from 1981–2024, mostly in the last few seasons, and none of them is a podium.\n4) **The remaining grid 0 means a pit-lane start:** these drivers raced but did not start from a grid slot, which is how the F1 introduction describes grid 0. As a number, 0 would look better than pole position, so in Section 3 we must recode it as starting from the back rather than leave it as 0.\n\n**Summary of Part C:** the cleaning removed rows that were never predictions (non-starters, the Indianapolis 500 and second rows from shared drives). What remains is one row per driver who actually raced, with a podium share that is much closer to what each era's field size implies.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n# 2. Data-Engineering Questions\n\nThe brief asks three questions that can only be answered by combining several tables. For each one we give the answer, a plot, and a short note on grain, missingness and, where relevant, the pre-race cutoff.\n\nAll three start from the cleaned `results` from Part B: one row per driver who raced in a race. These are descriptions of past races, not features, so they may use post-race columns such as the finishing position or the status. The pre-race restriction applies to the features we build in Section 3.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Q1 · Front-row conversion by circuit, before and after 2014\n\n**Question:** which circuits convert a front-row start into a podium most often, and does that ranking change after the 2014 hybrid era? The brief asks us to distinguish qualifying position from grid.\n\n**Tables:** `results` → `races` (season and circuit) → `circuits` (circuit name), plus `qualifying` (qualifying position), joined on race and driver. 1.6 showed that `qualifying` has one row per driver per race, so this join cannot add rows.\n\n**Our definitions**\n\n1) **Front-row start:** grid 1 or 2, where the car actually started; or qualifying position 1 or 2, where the driver qualified. The F1 introduction explains why the two can differ: penalties move drivers back, and in 2021–2022 the sprint set the grid on sprint weekends.\n2) **Conversion rate:** the share of a circuit's front-row starters who finished on the podium.\n3) **Eras:** before 2014 vs 2014 onward. The 2022–2024 seasons are counted in \"2014 onward\", because the question asks whether the ranking changed from 2014.\n4) **Time windows:** grid is known for every season, so we compare 1950–2013 with 2014–2024. Qualifying is only complete from 2003 (plot 3 in 1.3), so for qualifying we compare 2003–2013 with 2014–2024.\n5) **Minimum sample:** a circuit is ranked only if it has at least 10 front-row starts in each era, about five races. With fewer, a single result moves the rate by more than 10 points.\n\nFirst we build the table and check it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# cleaned results + season, circuit and qualifying position (also reused in Q2 and Q3)\nbase = (clean[\"results\"]\n        .merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"circuitId\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\n        .merge(clean[\"circuits\"][[\"circuitId\", \"name\", \"country\"]].rename(columns={\"name\": \"circuit\"}),\n               on=\"circuitId\", how=\"left\")\n        .merge(clean[\"qualifying\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"position\"]].rename(columns={\"position\": \"quali_pos\"}),\n               on=[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], how=\"left\"))\nbase[\"podium\"] = base[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3\nbase[\"era\"] = np.where(base[\"year\"] >= 2014, \"2014+\", \"before 2014\")\nprint(\"Rows before and after the joins:\", len(clean[\"results\"]), len(base))\n\n# does every race have exactly two front-row starters on the grid?\nfront_per_race = base[base[\"grid\"].isin([1, 2])].groupby(\"raceId\").size()\nprint(\"\\nFront-row starters (grid 1 or 2) per race:\")\nprint(front_per_race.value_counts().sort_index())\nprint(\"Races with no front-row starter at all:\", base[\"raceId\"].nunique() - len(front_per_race))\n\n# from 2003 qualifying is complete: how often do qualifying and grid disagree on the front row?\nmodern = base[base[\"year\"] >= 2003]\nprint(\"\\nRows from 2003 without a qualifying position:\", modern[\"quali_pos\"].isna().sum(), \"of\", len(modern))\nprint(pd.crosstab(modern[\"quali_pos\"].isin([1, 2]), modern[\"grid\"].isin([1, 2]),\n                  rownames=[\"qualified 1-2\"], colnames=[\"started 1-2\"]))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.444047Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.444809Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.500856Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.444772Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.500019Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows before and after the joins: 24524 24524\n\nFront-row starters (grid 1 or 2) per race:\n1      14\n2    1098\n4       1\nName: count, dtype: int64\nRaces with no front-row starter at all: 1\n\nRows from 2003 without a qualifying position: 21 of 8910\nstarted 1-2    False  True \nqualified 1-2              \nFalse           8022     36\nTrue              37    815\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":58},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **The joins kept every row:** 24,524 before and after, so adding season, circuit and qualifying position created no duplicates.\n2) **Almost every race has exactly two front-row starters:** 1,098 of the 1,114 races. 14 have only one, one has none (the 2005 United States Grand Prix, where only 6 drivers remain after removing non-starters) and one has four (the 1952 Italian Grand Prix, where two drivers are recorded on grid 1 and two on grid 2). These 16 races are too few to change the rates noticeably.\n3) **Qualifying is almost complete from 2003:** only 21 of 8,910 rows from 2003 have no qualifying position.\n4) **Qualifying and grid usually agree on the front row, but not always:** of the 852 drivers who qualified 1st or 2nd since 2003, 37 did not start from the front row, and 36 drivers started on the front row without qualifying there. The F1 introduction gives the reasons: penalties, and the sprint setting the grid in 2021–2022. This is the difference the brief asks us to keep apart, so we compute the conversion both ways.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Conversion rate per circuit.** We compute the rate for each circuit and era, once with grid and once with qualifying position, keep the circuits with enough starts in both eras, and measure how much the ranking changed with a rank correlation between the two eras. 1 would mean the order of circuits stayed exactly the same, 0 that it is unrelated.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"MIN_STARTS = 10   # front-row starts a circuit needs in each era to be ranked\n\ndef conversion(rows, start_col):\n    \"\"\"Podium rate of front-row starters (start_col 1 or 2), per circuit and era.\"\"\"\n    front = rows[rows[start_col].isin([1, 2])]\n    table = front.pivot_table(index=\"circuit\", columns=\"era\", values=\"podium\", aggfunc=[\"size\", \"mean\"])\n    table.columns = [(\"starts \" if agg == \"size\" else \"rate \") + era for agg, era in table.columns]\n    enough = (table[\"starts before 2014\"] >= MIN_STARTS) & (table[\"starts 2014+\"] >= MIN_STARTS)\n    return table[enough].sort_values(\"rate 2014+\", ascending=False)\n\nviews = {\"Grid 1-2 (1950-2013 vs 2014-2024)\": conversion(base, \"grid\"),\n         \"Qualifying 1-2 (2003-2013 vs 2014-2024)\": conversion(modern, \"quali_pos\")}\n\nfor title, t in views.items():\n    print(title, \"| circuits ranked:\", len(t),\n          \"| rank correlation between eras:\", round(t[\"rate before 2014\"].corr(t[\"rate 2014+\"], method=\"spearman\"), 2))\n    display(t.round(2))\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(16, 0.35 * max(len(t) for t in views.values()) + 2))\nfor ax, (title, t) in zip(axes, views.items()):\n    t = t.sort_values(\"rate 2014+\")\n    y = np.arange(len(t))\n    ax.hlines(y, t[\"rate before 2014\"], t[\"rate 2014+\"], color=\"lightgrey\")\n    ax.scatter(t[\"rate before 2014\"], y, label=\"before 2014\")\n    ax.scatter(t[\"rate 2014+\"], y, label=\"2014+\")\n    ax.set_yticks(y)\n    ax.set_yticklabels(t.index)\n    ax.set_xlabel(\"share of front-row starters finishing on the podium\")\n    ax.set_title(title)\n    ax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.50226Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.502675Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:43.731577Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:42.502636Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:43.730432Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Grid 1-2 (1950-2013 vs 2014-2024) | circuits ranked: 16 | rank correlation between eras: -0.08\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                                starts 2014+  starts before 2014  rate 2014+  \\\ncircuit                                                                        \nCircuit de Monaco                       19.0               119.0        0.89   \nSuzuka Circuit                          18.0                50.0        0.89   \nYas Marina Circuit                      22.0                10.0        0.86   \nCircuit de Barcelona-Catalunya          22.0                45.0        0.82   \nCircuit de Spa-Francorchamps            22.0                92.0        0.82   \nAutódromo José Carlos Pace              19.0                62.0        0.79   \nShanghai International Circuit          14.0                20.0        0.79   \nCircuit Gilles Villeneuve               18.0                68.0        0.78   \nSilverstone Circuit                     24.0                94.0        0.75   \nRed Bull Ring                           26.0                50.0        0.73   \nAutodromo Nazionale di Monza            22.0               127.0        0.73   \nAlbert Park Grand Prix Circuit          18.0                36.0        0.72   \nMarina Bay Street Circuit               18.0                12.0        0.72   \nBahrain International Circuit           24.0                18.0        0.67   \nAutódromo Hermanos Rodríguez            18.0                30.0        0.61   \nHungaroring                             22.0                56.0        0.59   \n\n     