d>1</td>\n      <td>0.023</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"\n2022-2024: mechanical rate over all starts 0.059\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"       constructor  starts  mechanical   rate\n24      Alfa Romeo      88          11  0.125\n29  Alpine F1 Team     135          14  0.104\n28      AlphaTauri      88           7  0.080\n20        Williams     134           8  0.060\n21         Ferrari     134           7  0.052\n25    Aston Martin     134           7  0.052\n27    Haas F1 Team     135           7  0.052\n26        Mercedes     136           6  0.044\n30      RB F1 Team      48           2  0.042\n19         McLaren     136           5  0.037\n22        Red Bull     136           5  0.037\n23          Sauber      48           1  0.021","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>constructor</th>\n      <th>starts</th>\n      <th>mechanical</th>\n      <th>rate</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24</th>\n      <td>Alfa Romeo</td>\n      <td>88</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>0.125</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>29</th>\n      <td>Alpine F1 Team</td>\n      <td>135</td>\n      <td>14</td>\n      <td>0.104</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>28</th>\n      <td>AlphaTauri</td>\n      <td>88</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>0.080</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>20</th>\n      <td>Williams</td>\n      <td>134</td>\n      <td>8</td>\n      <td>0.060</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>21</th>\n      <td>Ferrari</td>\n      <td>134</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>0.052</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>25</th>\n      <td>Aston Martin</td>\n      <td>134</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>0.052</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>27</th>\n      <td>Haas F1 Team</td>\n      <td>135</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>0.052</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>26</th>\n      <td>Mercedes</td>\n      <td>136</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>0.044</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>30</th>\n      <td>RB F1 Team</td>\n      <td>48</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>0.042</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>19</th>\n      <td>McLaren</td>\n      <td>136</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>0.037</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>22</th>\n      <td>Red Bull</td>\n      <td>136</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>0.037</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>23</th>\n      <td>Sauber</td>\n      <td>48</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>0.021</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x600 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":64},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **2014–2021, highest rates:** Caterham (21.2%), Lotus F1 (18.7%) and Toro Rosso (16.1%), followed by Renault (14.1%) and McLaren (13.6%). Caterham and Lotus F1 have few starts (33 and 75), so their rates are less certain. Among the constructors that raced through the whole period (over 300 starts), McLaren has the highest rate and Mercedes the lowest (4.1%).\n2) **2022–2024, highest rates:** Alfa Romeo (12.5%), Alpine F1 Team (10.4%) and AlphaTauri (8.0%). The lowest are Sauber (2.1%, only 48 starts), and Red Bull and McLaren (3.7% each).\n3) **The ranking changed:** McLaren went from among the least reliable to joint second-best (13.6% → 3.7%), and Red Bull from 10.7% to 3.7%. Mercedes was among the most reliable in both periods (4.1% and 4.4%).\n4) **One team lineage stays near the top:** the F1 introduction names Toleman → Benetton → Renault → Lotus → Alpine as one team under changing names. Its recent names are all high: Lotus F1 (18.7%) and Renault (14.1%) in 2014–2021, and Alpine (10.4%) in 2022–2024. Because we group by `constructorId`, as the introduction advises, these appear as separate constructors.\n5) **Differences of a point or two are noise:** with about 135 starts per team in 2022–2024, one extra failure moves a rate by about 0.7 points (2 points for teams with 48 starts). Ferrari, Aston Martin and Haas at 5.2% (7 failures each) cannot be told apart.\n\n**Answer to Q3:** in 2014–2021 the highest mechanical-retirement rates belong to Caterham (21.2%), Lotus F1 (18.7%) and Toro Rosso (16.1%). Among full-period teams, McLaren is the highest (13.6%). In 2022–2024 they belong to Alfa Romeo (12.5%), Alpine F1 Team (10.4%) and AlphaTauri (8.0%). Mechanical failures became less common overall (9.5% → 5.9% of starts), and the ranking changed: McLaren and Red Bull moved from the upper half to the most reliable teams, while Mercedes stayed reliable in both periods.\n\n**Grain, missingness and cutoff**\n- **Grain:** one row per driver per race start (cleaned), aggregated per constructor (`constructorId`) and period. Each car counts separately, so a two-car team has about twice as many starts as races.\n- **Missingness:** `status` is complete. 49 retirements have only `Retired` and no cause; we count them as unknown, which can lower a team's mechanical rate by at most a few failures.\n- **Mapping choices:** 22 debatable failures (`Wheel`, `Wheel nut`, wings, `Vibrations`) are counted as mechanical and 12 tyre-damage endings (`Puncture`, `Tyre`, `Debris`) as not mechanical. The full mapping is in the code above.\n- **Renamed teams:** teams that changed name appear separately, and some names cover only part of a period (for example Alpine F1 Team, 44 starts in 2014–2021).\n- **Pre-race cutoff:** `status` is post-race. Q3 describes past races and uses it as the outcome, so this is not leakage. In Section 3 a team's reliability could only be used from earlier races.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n# 3. Feature Selection & Engineering\n\nIn this section we turn the cleaned results into the modeling table: one row per driver per race, with only information that is known after qualifying and before the race starts. Every feature has to pass two tests. \n    \n* It must be available at that cutoffto prevent leakage\n* and our data must show that it relates to finishing on the podium.\n\n\nWe first decide which seasons the model learns from, then build the features one group at a time (grid, qualifying, standings and form), each with the evidence behind it, and finally split the data into training, validation and test seasons.\n\nBecause the window decides which features are possible, and how much of each feature is missing since they change overtime ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.1 Which seasons do we model?\n\nPlot 3 in 1.3 showed that qualifying data does not exist before 1994 and covers only part of the races in 1996–2002. The F1 introduction lists three ways to deal with this: impute qualifying, fall back to `grid`, or restrict the training window. Before choosing, we measure what each starting season would give us: how many rows, how many of them have a qualifying position, and the podium rate.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"rows = (clean[\"results\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"positionOrder\"]]\n        .merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\n        .merge(clean[\"qualifying\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"position\"]], on=[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], how=\"left\"))\n\nwindow = []\nfor start in [1950, 1994, 2003]:\n    w = rows[rows[\"year\"] >= start]\n    window.append({\"seasons\": f\"{start}-2024\", \"rows\": len(w),\n                   \"rows with qualifying (%)\": round(100 * w[\"position\"].notna().mean(), 1),\n                   \"podium rate (%)\": round(100 * (w[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).mean(), 1)})\ndisplay(pd.DataFrame(window))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.816231Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.816532Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.846711Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.816496Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.845863Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"     seasons   rows  rows with qualifying (%)  podium rate (%)\n0  1950-2024  24524                      42.5             13.6\n1  1994-2024  12219                      85.3             14.2\n2  2003-2024   8910                      99.8             14.4","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>seasons</th>\n      <th>rows</th>\n      <th>rows with qualifying (%)</th>\n      <th>podium rate (%)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>1950-2024</td>\n      <td>24524</td>\n      <td>42.5</td>\n      <td>13.6</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>1994-2024</td>\n      <td>12219</td>\n      <td>85.3</td>\n      <td>14.2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>2003-2024</td>\n      <td>8910</td>\n      <td>99.8</td>\n      <td>14.4</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":65},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Qualifying coverage decides the window:** over all seasons only 42.5% of rows have a qualifying position, so a qualifying feature would have to be guessed for most of the table. From 1994 it is still missing for about one row in seven. From 2003 it is missing for only 21 of 8,910 rows (99.8% complete).\n2) **Podiums are just as common in every window:** the podium rate is the number of podium rows divided by all rows. Since every race has three podium places, it equals three divided by the average number of starters per race. It is 13.6% for 1950–2024, 14.2% for 1994–2024 and 14.4% for 2003–2024 (1,284 podiums in 428 races with about 21 starters each), so keeping only 2003–2024 does not make podiums rarer or more common in our data.\n3) **The split fits inside the window:** the F1 introduction gives the season boundaries train ≤ 2019, validation 2020–2021 and test ≥ 2022. A window starting in 2003 contains all three, each with complete qualifying data.\n\n**The three options from the F1 introduction**\n\n1) **Impute qualifying:** keep every season and fill in the missing qualifying positions with estimates. Rejected: for most of the history we would be inventing the very information we want the model to learn from.\n2) **Fall back to grid:** use the starting position where qualifying is missing. `grid` is complete for every season and, as 1.1 showed, it is known before the race, so it will be one of our features anyway. Falling back can mean two things, and neither works well:\n   - copying `grid` into the qualifying column for older races, which gives the model nothing it does not already get from `grid` itself, or\n   - dropping qualifying and keeping every season, which loses what qualifying adds on top of the grid: in Q1, 37 drivers since 2003 qualified on the front row but did not start there.\n3) **Restrict the training window:** keep only the seasons where qualifying is complete. **Chosen.**\n\n**Our decision:** the model learns from the 2003–2024 seasons. The cost is fewer rows (8,910 instead of 24,524), but the seasons we keep are also closer to the 2022–2024 test seasons in field size, reliability and rules.\n\n**What this does not change:** form and standings features look back at earlier races. A 2003 race may still use results from 2002, so these features are built from the full cleaned history and only the rows we predict are limited to 2003–2024.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.2 The base table\n\nWe start from the cleaned `results` (one row per driver who started a race) and attach what every later step needs: the season, round and date of the race from `races`, and the driver's qualifying position from `qualifying`. The driver's team (`constructorId`) is taken from `results`, as decided in 1.1, since it records the team the driver actually raced for.\n\nWe keep all cleaned seasons here, not only 2003–2024. Form and standings features look back at earlier races, so a 2003 race must be able to see what happened in 2002. The rows are sorted by race date, because 1.1 showed that IDs are not in time order and every \"earlier races\" feature depends on the correct order.\n\n`positionOrder` stays in the table only to define the target and to describe past races. It is never used as a feature for the race it belongs to.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"history = (clean[\"results\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"constructorId\", \"grid\", \"positionOrder\"]]\n           .merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"round\", \"date\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\n           .merge(clean[\"qualifying\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"position\"]].rename(columns={\"position\": \"quali_pos\"}),\n                  on=[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], how=\"left\"))\nhistory[\"podium\"] = (history[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).astype(int)\nhistory = history.sort_values([\"date\", \"raceId\"]).reset_index(drop=True)\n\nprint(\"Rows before and after the joins:\", len(clean[\"results\"]), len(history))\nprint(\"Duplicated (raceId, driverId):\", history.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]).sum())\nprint(\"Races sharing a date with another race:\", history.groupby(\"raceId\")[\"date\"].first().duplicated().sum())\nprint(\"Seasons:\", history[\"year\"].min(), \"-\", history[\"year\"].max(),\n      \"| rows from 2003:\", (history[\"year\"] >= 2003).sum())\ndisplay(history.tail())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.853138Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.85351Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.901081Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.853482Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.900277Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows before and after the joins: 24524 24524\nDuplicated (raceId, driverId): 0\nRaces sharing a date with another race: 0\nSeasons: 1950 - 2024 | rows from 2003: 8910\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"       raceId  driverId  constructorId  grid  positionOrder  year  round  \\\n24519    1144       825            210    14             16  2024     24   \n24520    1144       859            215    12             17  2024     24   \n24521    1144       822             15     9             18  2024     24   \n24522    1144       861              3    20             19  2024     24   \n24523    1144       815              9    10             20  2024     24   \n\n            date  quali_pos  podium  \n24519 2024-12-08       15.0       0  \n24520 2024-12-08       12.0       0  \n24521 2024-12-08        9.0       0  \n24522 2024-12-08       19.0       0  \n24523 2024-12-08       10.0       0  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>raceId</th>\n      <th>driverId</th>\n      <th>constructorId</th>\n      <th>grid</th>\n      <th>positionOrder</th>\n      <th>year</th>\n      <th>round</th>\n      <th>date</th>\n      <th>quali_pos</th>\n      <th>podium</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24519</th>\n      <td>1144</td>\n      <td>825</td>\n      <td>210</td>\n      <td>14</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>2024</td>\n      <td>24</td>\n      <td>2024-12-08</td>\n      <td>15.0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24520</th>\n      <td>1144</td>\n      <td>859</td>\n      <td>215</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>2024</td>\n      <td>24</td>\n      <td>2024-12-08</td>\n      <td>12.0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24521</th>\n      <td>1144</td>\n      <td>822</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>2024</td>\n      <td>24</td>\n      <td>2024-12-08</td>\n      <td>9.0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24522</th>\n      <td>1144</td>\n      <td>861</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>19</td>\n      <td>2024</td>\n      <td>24</td>\n      <td>2024-12-08</td>\n      <td>19.0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24523</th>\n      <td>1144</td>\n      <td>815</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>2024</td>\n      <td>24</td>\n      <td>2024-12-08</td>\n      <td>10.0</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":66},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **The joins kept every row:** 24,524 before and after, and no driver appears twice in a race, so the table still has the grain we fixed in 1.7.\n2) **Time order is well defined:** no two races share a date, so sorting by `date` puts every race in a single, clear order. This is the order every \"earlier races\" feature in this section relies on.\n3) **History and modeling rows:** the table covers 1950–2024. The 8,910 rows from 2003 onward form the modeling table, later split into training (2003–2019), validation (2020–2021) and test (2022–2024) seasons. The earlier rows are kept only as history for features that look back at past races.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.3 Grid\n\n`grid` is the position a car actually starts from, after penalties, and 1.1 showed it is known before the race. It is complete, but it contains the value 0, which 1.3 and 1.9 showed now means a pit-lane start: after cleaning, only drivers who really raced from the pit lane are left with grid 0. As a number, 0 would look better than pole position, so we recode it as a start from the back of the field, using the number of starters in that race.\n\nTo keep the validation and test seasons unseen, the evidence for every feature in this section comes only from the training seasons, 2003–2019.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# grid 0 is a pit-lane start (1.3, 1.9): count it as starting from the back of the field\nhistory[\"starters\"] = history.groupby(\"raceId\")[\"driverId\"].transform(\"size\")\nhistory[\"grid_pos\"] = history[\"grid\"].where(history[\"grid\"] > 0, history[\"starters\"])\n\nmodel_rows = history[\"year\"] >= 2003\nprint(\"Pit-lane starts (grid 0) from 2003:\", (history.loc[model_rows, \"grid\"] == 0).sum(),\n      \"| podiums among them:\", history.loc[model_rows & (history[\"grid\"] == 0), \"podium\"].sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.902087Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.902369Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.918422Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.902344Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.917054Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Pit-lane starts (grid 0) from 2003: 73 | podiums among them: 0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":67},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# evidence only from the training seasons (2003-2019), so validation and test stay unseen\ntrain_rows = (history[\"year\"] >= 2003) & (history[\"year\"] <= 2019)\nby_grid = history[train_rows].groupby(\"grid_pos\")[\"podium\"].mean()\n\nax = (100 * by_grid).plot(marker=\"o\", figsize=(9, 4))\nax.set_xlabel(\"starting position (pit lane counted as last)\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"podium rate (%)\")\nax.set_title(\"Podium rate by starting position, training seasons 2003-2019\")\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Podium rate from grid 1, 2, 3 (%):\", (100 * by_grid.loc[1:3]).round(1).tolist())\nprint(\"Spearman correlation, grid_pos vs podium:\",\n      round(history.loc[train_rows, \"grid_pos\"].corr(history.loc[train_rows, \"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.919641Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.919957Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.103161Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:44.919931Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.102066Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Podium rate from grid 1, 2, 3 (%): [80.3, 62.7, 51.7]\nSpearman correlation, grid_pos vs podium: -0.494\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":68},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Starting position is strongly linked to a podium:** in the training seasons, 80.3% of drivers starting from pole finished on the podium, 62.7% from 2nd and 51.7% from 3rd. The rate falls quickly after that, to about 5% from 8th place, and is close to zero from 12th backwards. The rank correlation between starting position and podium is −0.49: the further back a driver starts, the less likely a podium.\n2) **This matches the F1 introduction:** it calls pole position the \"strongest single predictor of a podium\", and our data agrees.\n3) **The recode is safe:** from 2003 there are 73 pit-lane starts and none of them ended on the podium, so counting them as starting last puts them where their results are.\n\n**Our decision:** `grid_pos` is our first feature, forming the grid group. The relationship is clearly not a straight line (the drop from 1st to 4th is far larger than from 10th to 13th), which matters later: a linear model may need help with this shape, while tree models handle it on their own.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.4 Qualifying\n\n`quali_pos` is the driver's position in the qualifying session, known before the race. 1.1 showed it is not the same as `grid`: penalties can move a driver back, and in 2021–2022 the sprint set the grid on sprint weekends. We check two things on the training seasons: whether the qualifying position is linked to a podium, and whether it tells us anything that `grid_pos` does not already tell us.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"t = history[train_rows]\nby_quali = t.groupby(\"quali_pos\")[\"podium\"].mean()\n\nax = (100 * by_grid).plot(marker=\"o\", figsize=(9, 4), label=\"by starting position (grid)\")\n(100 * by_quali).plot(ax=ax, marker=\"s\", label=\"by qualifying position\")\nax.set_xlabel(\"position\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"podium rate (%)\")\nax.set_title(\"Podium rate by grid and by qualifying position, training seasons 2003-2019\")\nax.legend()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Rows without a qualifying position:\", t[\"quali_pos\"].isna().sum(), \"of\", len(t))\nprint(\"Spearman with podium: qualifying\", round(t[\"quali_pos\"].corr(t[\"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3),\n      \"| grid\", round(t[\"grid_pos\"].corr(t[\"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3))\nprint(\"Spearman between qualifying and grid:\", round(t[\"quali_pos\"].corr(t[\"grid_pos\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.104575Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.105128Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.319751Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.105083Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.318635Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows without a qualifying position: 20 of 6783\nSpearman with podium: qualifying -0.497 | grid -0.494\nSpearman between qualifying and grid: 0.97\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":69},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"the only differnce is when slower drivers atrted ahed because a better driver received penalties and started after them but still managed to win them ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# compare drivers who start from the same grid group: did they qualify higher than they start?\nt = history[train_rows & history[\"quali_pos\"].notna()].copy()\nt[\"grid_group\"] = pd.cut(t[\"grid_pos\"], bins=[0, 3, 6, 10, 30], labels=[\"1-3\", \"4-6\", \"7-10\", \"11+\"])\nt[\"start_vs_quali\"] = np.where(t[\"grid_pos\"] > t[\"quali_pos\"], \"started behind qualifying\",\n                               \"started at or ahead of qualifying\")\ndisplay(t.groupby([\"grid_group\", \"start_vs_quali\"], observed=True)[\"podium\"].agg([\"size\", \"mean\"]).round(3))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.320798Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.321102Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.34637Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.321076Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:34:45.345423Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                                              size   mean\ngrid_group start_vs_quali                                \n1-3        started at or ahead of qualifying   958  0.649\n4-6        started at or ahead of qualifying   949  0.230\n           started behind qualifying            12  0.083\n7-10       started at or ahead of qualifying  1234  0.054\n           started behind qualifying            44  0.159\n11+        started at or ahead of qualifying  3228  0.011\n           started behind qualifying           338  0.033","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th>size</th>\n      <th>mean</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>grid_group</th>\n      <th>start_vs_quali</th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1-3</th>\n      <th>started at or ahead of qualifying</th>\n      <td>958</td>\n      <td>0.649</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th rowspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">4-6</th>\n      <th>started at or ahead of qualifying</th>\n      <td>949</td>\n      <td>0.230</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>started behind qualifying</th>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>0.083</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th rowspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">7-10</th>\n      <th>started at or ahead of qualifying</th>\n      <td>1234</td>\n      <td>0.054</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>started behind qualifying</th>\n      <td>44</td>\n      <td>0.159</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th rowspan=\"2\" valign=\"top\">11+</th>\n      <th>started at or ahead of qualifying</th>\n      <td>3228</td>\n      <td>0.011</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>started behind qualifying</th>\n      <td>338</td>\n      <td>0.033</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":70},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Qualifying is linked to a podium as strongly as the grid:** the two curves almost overlap (79.1% of pole-sitters in qualifying reached the podium, against 80.3% from pole on the grid), and the correlations with podium are almost equal (−0.497 for qualifying, −0.494 for grid). \n2) **The two are very similar:** their rank correlation is 0.97. For most drivers the qualifying position and the starting position are the same or almost the same.\n3) **But qualifying adds information when they differ:** among drivers starting 7th–10th, those who had qualified higher than they started reached the podium 15.9% of the time, against 5.4% for the others. From 11th or lower it is 3.3% against 1.1%. A driver moved back by a penalty is still faster than his starting slot suggests, and qualifying captures that. (The 4th–6th group has only 12 such drivers, too few to read anything into.)\n4) **Almost complete:** only 20 of the 6,783 training rows have no qualifying position. They are handled with the other missing values in 3.8.\n\n**Our decision:** `quali_pos` is the qualifying group. Together with `grid_pos`, the model can see both how fast a driver was in qualifying and where he actually starts, and therefore also the gap between the two.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.5 Rank before the race\n\nThe F1 introduction names car performance as the dominant factor in who wins, and the championship standings summarise how strong a driver and a team have been so far in the season. But 1.1 showed that the standings stored for a race are the standings after it: they already contain that race's points. Using them for the same race would be leakage. Following the F1 introduction, we shift them to \"standings before this race\": for every race we take the standings after the previous race of the same season. The first race of a season has no earlier standings, so those rows are left empty. 1.6 showed that both standings tables have one row per driver (or team) per race, so these joins cannot add rows.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# for every race, the previous race of the same season (none for the first race)\nraces = clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"round\"]].sort_values([\"year\", \"round\"])\nraces[\"prev_raceId\"] = races.groupby(\"year\")[\"raceId\"].shift(1)\nhistory = history.merge(races[[\"raceId\", \"prev_raceId\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\n\n# standings after the previous race = standings before this race\ndriver_before = clean[\"driver_standings\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"position\"]].rename(\n    columns={\"raceId\": \"prev_raceId\", \"position\": \"driver_rank_before\"})\nteam_before = clean[\"constructor_standings\"][[\"raceId\", \"constructorId\", \"position\"]].rename(\n    columns={\"raceId\": \"prev_raceId\", \"position\": \"team_rank_before\"})\n\nrows_before = len(history)\nhistory = (history.merge(driver_before, on=[\"prev_raceId\", \"driverId\"], how=\"left\")\n                  .merge(team_before, on=[\"prev_raceId\", \"constructorId\"], how=\"left\"))\nprint(\"Rows before and after the joins:\", rows_before, len(history))\n\nmodel_rows = history[\"year\"] >= 2003\nprint(\"Missing from 2003 (driver, team):\", history.loc[model_rows, \"driver_rank_before\"].isna().sum(),\n      history.loc[model_rows, \"team_rank_before\"].isna().sum(), \"of\", model_rows.sum(),\n      \"| rows in a season's first race:\", history.loc[model_rows, \"prev_raceId\"].isna().sum())\n\n# check the shift on one driver: Hamilton (driverId 1), first races of 2008\ndisplay(history[(history[\"year\"] == 2008) & (history[\"driverId\"] == 1)]\n        [[\"round\", \"positionOrder\", \"driver_rank_before\", \"team_rank_before\"]].head(4))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:41:24.60215Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:41:24.602548Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:41:24.655588Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:41:24.602521Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:41:24.654548Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows before and after the joins: 24524 24524\nMissing from 2003 (driver, team): 594 484 of 8910 | rows in a season's first race: 453\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"       round  positionOrder  driver_rank_before  team_rank_before\n17423      1              1                 NaN               NaN\n17449      2              5                 1.0               1.0\n17479      3             13                 1.0               1.0\n17491      4              3                 3.0               3.0","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>round</th>\n      <th>positionOrder</th>\n      <th>driver_rank_before</th>\n      <th>team_rank_before</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17423</th>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>NaN</td>\n      <td>NaN</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17449</th>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>1.0</td>\n      <td>1.0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17479</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>1.0</td>\n      <td>1.0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17491</th>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":71},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# history now has new columns, so rebuild the training-season mask\ntrain_rows = (history[\"year\"] >= 2003) & (history[\"year\"] <= 2019)\nt = history[train_rows]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\n(100 * t.groupby(\"driver_rank_before\")[\"podium\"].mean()).plot(ax=axes[0], marker=\"o\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Podium rate by driver's championship rank before the race\")\naxes[0].set_xlabel(\"driver's championship rank before the race\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"podium rate (%)\")\n(100 * t.groupby(\"team_rank_before\")[\"podium\"].mean()).plot(ax=axes[1], marker=\"o\", color=\"darkorange\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"Podium rate by team's championship rank before the race\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"team's championship rank before the race\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nfor col in [\"driver_rank_before\", \"team_rank_before\"]:\n    print(f\"Spearman with podium, {col}:\", round(t[col].corr(t[\"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3),\n          \"| with grid_pos:\", round(t[col].corr(t[\"grid_pos\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:50:31.153296Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:50:31.153735Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:50:31.500445Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:50:31.153693Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:50:31.499431Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Spearman with podium, driver_rank_before: -0.478 | with grid_pos: 0.726\nSpearman with podium, team_rank_before: -0.447 | with grid_pos: 0.708\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":73},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **The shift works:** Hamilton won the first race of 2008. His standing before that race is empty, and before the second race he is 1st, exactly the result of the first race. Every row only sees races that have already happened.\n2) **Strong drivers and teams reach the podium far more often:** in the training seasons, the driver leading the championship before a race finished on the podium 67.7% of the time, the 2nd 57.4% and the 3rd 41.5%. For teams it is 58.9% for the leading team, 40.0% for the 2nd, 26.3% for the 3rd, and only 4.3% for the 5th. The correlations with podium are −0.48 (driver) and −0.45 (team).\n3) **Related to the grid, but not the same:** the correlation with the starting position is about 0.7, far below the 0.97 between qualifying and grid. The standings describe the whole season so far, while the grid describes one Saturday, so they can add something to each other. The F1 introduction names car performance as the dominant factor, and the hybrid era shows that the constructor explains most of the variation in results, which supports keeping the team standing.\n4) **Missing values have a clear cause:** 594 of the 8,910 modeling rows have no driver standing and 484 no team standing. 453 of them are first races of a season, where no standings exist yet; the rest are drivers or teams with no standing entry before that race. They are handled in 3.8.\n\n**Our decision:** `driver_rank_before` and `team_rank_before` form the standings group.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.6 Recent form\n\nThe standings in 3.5 only cover the current season and are empty at its first race. Form fills that gap: it measures how often a driver, and separately his team, reached the podium in their most recent races, including races from the previous season.\n\nThe brief requires this feature to use only past races. We therefore sort all races by date and, for every row, look only at the races before it: the current race is never included. Driver form is the share of a driver's previous races that ended on the podium. Team form is the share of the team's cars that finished on the podium in the team's previous races. A driver or team with no earlier race has no form value.\n\nHow many past races should form cover? Too few and it reacts to luck, too many and it remembers seasons that no longer matter. We try 3, 5 and 10 races on the training seasons and keep the one most closely linked to a podium.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"history = history.sort_values([\"date\", \"raceId\"]).reset_index(drop=True)\ntrain_rows = (history[\"year\"] >= 2003) & (history[\"year\"] <= 2019)\n\ndef form(n):\n    \"\"\"Podium share over each driver's and each team's previous n races (never the current race).\"\"\"\n    past = lambda s: s.shift(1).rolling(n, min_periods=1).mean()\n    driver = history.groupby(\"driverId\")[\"podium\"].transform(past)\n    team_races = history.groupby([\"date\", \"raceId\", \"constructorId\"], as_index=False)[\"podium\"].mean()\n    team_races[\"team_form\"] = team_races.groupby(\"constructorId\")[\"podium\"].transform(past)\n    team = history[[\"raceId\", \"constructorId\"]].merge(team_races, on=[\"raceId\", \"constructorId\"], how=\"left\")[\"team_form\"]\n    return driver, team\n\ncompare = []\nfor n in [3, 5, 10]:\n    driver, team = form(n)\n    compare.append({\"previous races\": n,\n                    \"driver form vs podium\": round(driver[train_rows].corr(history.loc[train_rows, \"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3),\n                    \"team form vs podium\": round(team[train_rows].corr(history.loc[train_rows, \"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3)})\ndisplay(pd.DataFrame(compare))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:54:03.267382Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:54:03.267796Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:54:04.407459Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:54:03.267765Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:54:04.406556Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"   previous races  driver form vs podium  team form vs podium\n0               3                  0.511                0.513\n1               5                  0.526                0.515\n2              10                  0.519                0.502","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>previous races</th>\n      <th>driver form vs podium</th>\n      <th>team form vs podium</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>0.511</td>\n      <td>0.513</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>0.526</td>\n      <td>0.515</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>0.519</td>\n      <td>0.502</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":74},{"cell_type":"code","source":"history[\"driver_form\"], history[\"team_form\"] = form(5)\n\nmodel_rows = history[\"year\"] >= 2003\nprint(\"Missing from 2003 (driver, team):\", history.loc[model_rows, \"driver_form\"].isna().sum(),\n      history.loc[model_rows, \"team_form\"].isna().sum(), \"of\", model_rows.sum())\n\n# check on one driver: Hamilton (driverId 1) in 2008\ndisplay(history[(history[\"year\"] == 2008) & (history[\"driverId\"] == 1)][[\"round\", \"podium\", \"driver_form\"]].head(6))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:54:17.413607Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:54:17.413971Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:54:17.742034Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:54:17.413944Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:54:17.741274Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Missing from 2003 (driver, team): 89 41 of 8910\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"       round  podium  driver_form\n17423      1       1          0.4\n17449      2       0          0.4\n17479      3       0          0.4\n17491      4       1          0.2\n17512      5       1          0.4\n17531      6       1          0.6","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>round</th>\n      <th>podium</th>\n      <th>driver_form</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17423</th>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>0.4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17449</th>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0.4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17479</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>0.4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17491</th>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>0.2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17512</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>0.4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17531</th>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>0.6</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":75},{"cell_type":"code","source":"t = history[train_rows]\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\n(100 * t.groupby((t[\"driver_form\"] * 5).round() / 5)[\"podium\"].mean()).plot(ax=axes[0], marker=\"o\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Podium rate by driver form (last 5 races)\")\naxes[0].set_xlabel(\"share of the driver's last 5 races on the podium\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"podium rate (%)\")\n(100 * t.groupby(t[\"team_form\"].round(1))[\"podium\"].mean()).plot(ax=axes[1], marker=\"o\", color=\"darkorange\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"Podium rate by team form (last 5 races)\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"share of the team's cars on the podium in its last 5 races\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nfor col in [\"driver_form\", \"team_form\"]:\n    print(f\"Spearman with podium, {col}:\", round(t[col].corr(t[\"podium\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3),\n          \"| with grid_pos:\", round(t[col].corr(t[\"grid_pos\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3),\n          \"| with team_rank_before:\", round(t[col].corr(t[\"team_rank_before\"], method=\"spearman\"), 3))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T15:57:17.341261Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T15:57:17.341811Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T15:57:17.728522Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T15:57:17.341777Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T15:57:17.727458Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Spearman with podium, driver_form: 0.526 | with grid_pos: -0.569 | with team_rank_before: -0.683\nSpearman with podium, team_form: 0.515 | with grid_pos: -0.613 | with team_rank_before: -0.768\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":76},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Five past races work best:** form over the last 5 races is slightly more closely linked to a podium than 3 or 10 races (0.526 against 0.511 and 0.519 for drivers), so we use 5. The differences are small, which also means the exact choice is not critical.\n2) **Only past races are used:** Hamilton's form before the first race of 2008 is 0.4, his 2 podiums in the last 5 races of 2007, and it does not change because he won that race: the race itself is never counted. Form also carries over from one season to the next, which the standings cannot do.\n3) **Form is strongly linked to a podium:** in the training seasons, a driver with no podium in his last 5 races reached the podium only 2.9% of the time, against 69.1% for a driver with 5 out of 5. The correlations with podium (0.53 for drivers, 0.52 for teams) are as strong as for the grid.\n4) **It overlaps with the other groups but does not copy them:** form is related to the grid (about −0.6) and to the team standing (−0.68 and −0.77), since good teams start at the front and score points. But none of these correlations is close to the 0.97 between qualifying and grid, so form still carries its own information about recent results.\n5) **Few missing values:** 89 rows have no driver form and 41 no team form, from a driver's or a team's first race, where no earlier race exists. They are handled in 3.8.\n\n**Our decision:** `driver_form` and `team_form` form the form group.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.7 The feature set and the split\n\nSections 3.3–3.6 give six features in four groups, all known before the race:\n\n| Group | Features | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Grid | `grid_pos` | 3.3: podium rate falls from 80.3% on pole to about 5% from 8th |\n| Qualifying | `quali_pos` | 3.4: as strong as the grid, and adds information when a penalty moves a driver back |\n| Standings | `driver_rank_before`, `team_rank_before` | 3.5: championship leaders reach the podium 67.7% (driver) and 58.9% (team) of the time |\n| Form | `driver_form`, `team_form` | 3.6: no podium in the last 5 races gives 2.9%, five out of five gives 69.1% |\n\nTwo candidates are left out on purpose. A home-race feature is not included because Q2 found no home advantage once starting position is taken into account. Sprint results are not used because, as noted in 1.1, they exist only from 2021, so the training seasons contain none.\n\nWe split the 2003–2024 rows by season, following the boundaries in the F1 introduction: training 2003–2019, validation 2020–2021 and test 2022–2024. A split by time, rather than a random one, matches how the model would be used: it learns from past seasons and predicts later ones. A random split would put rows from the same race, and from later seasons, into training, so the model would partly be tested on races it had already seen. The validation seasons are used to choose settings and the decision threshold, and the test seasons are used only once, for the final scores.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"FEATURE_GROUPS = {\"grid\": [\"grid_pos\"], \"qualifying\": [\"quali_pos\"],\n                  \"standings\": [\"driver_rank_before\", \"team_rank_before\"],\n                  \"form\": [\"driver_form\", \"team_form\"]}\nFEATURES = [f for group in FEATURE_GROUPS.values() for f in group]\n\nmodel_table = history[history[\"year\"] >= 2003][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"year\"] + FEATURES + [\"podium\"]].copy()\nmodel_table[\"split\"] = np.select([model_table[\"year\"] <= 2019, model_table[\"year\"] <= 2021],\n                                 [\"train\", \"validation\"], default=\"test\")\n\nsummary = model_table.groupby(\"split\", sort=False).agg(seasons=(\"year\", lambda y: f\"{y.min()}-{y.max()}\"),\n                                                        races=(\"raceId\", \"nunique\"), rows=(\"podium\", \"size\"),\n                                                        podium_rate=(\"podium\", \"mean\"))\nsummary[\"podium_rate\"] = (100 * summary[\"podium_rate\"]).round(1)\ndisplay(summary)\nprint(\"Races in more than one split:\", (model_table.groupby(\"raceId\")[\"split\"].nunique() > 1).sum())\nprint(\"Missing values per split (%):\")\ndisplay((100 * model_table.groupby(\"split\", sort=False)[FEATURES].apply(lambda g: g.isna().mean())).round(1))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T16:03:58.748729Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T16:03:58.749127Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T16:03:58.807815Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T16:03:58.749098Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T16:03:58.806726Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"              seasons  races  rows  podium_rate\nsplit                                          \ntrain       2003-2019    321  6783         14.2\nvalidation  2020-2021     39   775         15.1\ntest        2022-2024     68  1352         15.1","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>seasons</th>\n      <th>races</th>\n      <th>rows</th>\n      <th>podium_rate</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>split</th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>train</th>\n