td>0.5</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>validation</th>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.1</td>\n      <td>5.5</td>\n      <td>5.2</td>\n      <td>0.8</td>\n      <td>0.5</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>test</th>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>5.0</td>\n      <td>4.4</td>\n      <td>0.6</td>\n      <td>0.1</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":77},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**What we notice**\n\n1) **Sizes:** training has 6,783 rows from 321 races (76% of the modeling table), validation 775 rows from 39 races, and test 1,352 rows from 68 races. No race is split across two sets.\n2) **Podiums are equally common in every split:** 14.2% in training and 15.1% in validation and test, close to the 3-out-of-20 of a modern grid. The model is evaluated on the same kind of target it learns from.\n3) **Missing values are few and of one main kind:** apart from the standings (5–7%, mostly first races of a season), every feature is missing for at most about 1% of rows. These are handled in 3.8.\n4) **Using earlier test races as history is not leakage:** the form and standings of a 2023 race use results from 2022 races, which belong to the test set. At prediction time those races are already over, so a real forecast would know them too. What the model never sees is the result of the race it predicts.\n5) **The test seasons are a new era:** 2022 brought new car rules (the ground-effect era in the F1 introduction), so the test checks whether what the model learned in 2003–2019 still holds. We report this as a limitation.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}